Members of the Wales National Assembly concerned that they ‘will be compared to Muppets’
There is a stir among the Members of the National Assembly of Wales by a proposal that their title would be changed to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament - Member of the Welsh Parliament).
The proposal is a consequence of the plans to change the current name ‘Welsh Assembly‘ to ‘Welsh Parliament‘.
Members of the National Assembly of various political parties are concerned that the new name comes across as ridiculous.
A member of the Lavor Party's National Assembly said MWP 'rhymes on Twp and Pwp'.
For readers outside Wales: twp is Welsh crazy and pwp is Welsh for poop.
A member of the Plaid Party's National Assembly of the Plaid Party said the group as a whole is "dissatisfied" and has proposed alternatives.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said his party is ‘open’ to the name change, but notes that the words MWP and Muppet are very similar.
In this context, the Welsh letter W is pronounced as a short you.
The Committee of the Wales National Assembly, which is currently drafting legislation to implement the name change, said: ‘It is, of course, the members of the Wales National Assembly themselves who make the final decision on their new title.’
With the Government of Wales Act 2017, the members of the National Assembly of Wales were given the right to change the name of the Assembly.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposals, which showed that most people felt that the National Assembly should be renamed Parliament.
With regard to the title of the members of the National Assembly, the Commission proposed ‘Welsh Parliament Members’ or WMPs, but the MWP option received the most support in the public consultation.
At present, the members of the National Assembly would propose alternatives, but President Elin Jones will have a hard time reaching a consensus. She will have to submit a draft law on the changes in several weeks.
Legislation on the reforms also includes other amendments to the way the National Assembly works, including rules on the suspension of members of the National Assembly and the structure of the system of committees.
The members of the National Assembly will be able to vote definitively on their new title when they debate the legislation.
Citizens of Macedonia vote in a referendum on changing their country's name
On Sunday, voters vote whether they want to change their country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia."
The popular referendum was organised in an effort to resolve a lingering dispute with neighboring Greece, which itself has a province called Macedonia.
Athens has long claimed that the name of its northern neighbor means that the country would claim its territory and has already repeatedly objected to applications from Macedonia to join the EU and NATO.
Macedonia's President Gjorge Ivanov, who opposes the plebiscite on the name change, has said that he will not take into account the result of the referendum.
Proponents of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, however, argue that the name change is simply the price to be paid to join the EU and NATO.
The bells Saint Martin’s silence, Harlem’s are having a hard time
“The older people I’ve spoken to said there used to be a café and a church located here on every corner,” Mr. Adams said.
Today there is neither a cafe nor a church here.’
He said it's understandable that cafes have disappeared.
"People now have social contact in a different way," he said.
‘Cafés are no longer the living room of the neighborhood, where people visit regularly.’
However, he is concerned that the proceeds from the sale of the assets of the churches will last less than the leaders expect, “and sooner or later they stand back at the beginning.”
Churches, he adds, could be replaced by apartment buildings with flats where the kind of people would live that won't help in the neighborhood's remaining refuges.
“The vast majority of people who will buy flats in such buildings will be white,” he says, “which will cause these churches to be closed even faster, because it’s unlikely that the people moving to these flats will join the church community.”
Both churches were once built by a white church, before Harlem became a black metropolis: the metropolitan community in 1870, Saint Martin’s 10 years later.
The original church congregation of white methodists left in the 1930's.
A black congregation from the neighborhood took over the building.
Saint Martin's was taken over by a black church led by Reverend John Howard Johnson, who led a boycott of the shopkeepers in 125th Street, an important shopping street in Harlem, where blacks could only rarely get a job or be promoted.
In 1939 the building became serious damage from fire, but when the parishioners of Reverend Johnson made plans to rebuild the church, they also bought a carillon.
Reverend David Johnson, the son of Reverend Johnson and his successor in Saint Martin’s, proudly called the carillon “the bells of the poor.”
The expert who played the carillon in July referred in a different way to the instrument: ‘a cultural treasure’ and ‘an irreplaceable historical instrument’.
The expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also noted that this was the world's first carillon ever to be played by a black musician named Dionisio A. Lind, who moved 18 years ago to the larger carillon of Riverside Church.
Mr. Merriweather said Saint Martin's hadn't replaced him.
The events of the last few months at Saint Martin’s are a complicated story of architects and contractors, some hired by the church’s lay leaders, others by the diocese.
The consistory (the governing body of the parish, consisting of lay leaders) wrote the diocese in July to express its concern that the diocese ‘would like to pass on the costs’ to the consistory, even though the consistory had not been involved in hiring the architects and contractors the diocese had sent.
Some parishioners complained about the diocese's lack of transparency.
Shark injures 13-year-old who dived for lobsters in California
Saturday, on the first day of the lobster season, a shark attacked a 13-year-old boy who dived into lobsters in California. The boy suffered injuries, it reads.
The attack took place just before 7 a.m. near Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diegie that he had dived with friends on Saturday for about half an hour when he heard the boy screaming for help. Along with a few others, he paddled towards the boy to pull him out of the water.
Hammel said at first he thought the boy was calling because he had caught a lobster, but then he realized ‘that the boy shouted: ‘I’ve been bitten!
I’ve been bitten!”
His collarbone was completely open,” Hammel saw as he approached the boy.
“I shouted that everyone had to go out of the water: ‘There’s a shark in the water!’
The boy was taken by helicopter to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego, where he is reportedly in mortal danger.
It's not known which shark species is responsible for the attack.
Beaving Captain Larry Giles told a press conference that a shark had been spotted in the neighborhood a few weeks before, but that it had then been concluded that it was not a dangerous shark species.
Giles added that the victim was seriously injured in the upper body.
The authorities closed access to Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami in Ecinitas for 48 hours with a view to research and safety.
Giles noted that there are more than 135 shark species in the area, but that most are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's wants to break through at UK beauty market
Sainsbury's competes with Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams and establishes beauty departments where specialist assistants work, such as in department stores.
The initiative is part of a strategy to conquer the UK's beauty market, which is worth £2.8 billion and continues to grow, while sales of fashion and household items continue to decline. The larger beauty departments will be tested in 11 stores across the country. If they turn out to be a success, they will be introduced in even more stores next year.
The investment in the beauty market is also a result of the fact that supermarkets want to fill the shelves where TVs, microwaves and interior items once stood with other products.
Sainsbury’s wants to double its beauty offerings and offer 3,000 products, such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr PawPaw for the first time.
L'Oreal's, Maybelline and Burt's Bees is also gaining more space, with display space by brand, such as in stores such as Boots.
The supermarket is also bringing its Boutique make-up back to the market, now that most products are suitable for vegans, something younger shoppers are increasingly demanding.
In addition, perfumery the Fragrance Shop will test out concessions in two Sainsbury's stores. The first one opened in Croydon, in southern London, the second will open later this year in Selly Oak, in Birmingham.
As more and more people shop online and increasingly buy small amounts of food in local supermarkets on a daily basis, supermarkets need to make more effort to persuade people to shop with them.
Mike Coupe, Chief Executive of Sainsbury's, says the stores will look more and more as department stores, now that the supermarket chain is up against discounters Aldi and Lidl, with more services and non-food.
Sainsbury's has integrated Argos outlets into hundreds of stores and has also opened a number of Habitats since both chains were acquired two years ago. According to the company, food sales have risen as a result of this and the acquisitions have yielded more returns.
The last time that the supermarket was trying to give its departments Beauty and Pharmacy a make-over, that became a flop.
Sainsbury's tested a collaboration with Boots in the early 2000's, but the cooperation was terminated when a conflict arose over distributing revenues from the pharmacy department in the supermarkets.
This is a new strategy since Sainsbury's its 281 pharmacy departments in stores three years ago for an amount of 125 million British pounds sold to Celesio, owner of the chain Lloyds Pharmacy.
Lloyds would also play a role in the plan, by adding an extensive range of luxury brands for skincare, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy, in four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Commercial Director of Sainsburys, said: ‘We have completely changed the layout of our beauty department, so that it is more pleasant for our customers.
We have also invested in the specialized training of our employees, who will be ready to give advice.
With our range of brands we offer something for everyone, and thanks to the attractive environment and convenient locations, we ensure that our customers like to buy their beauty products from us, and no longer shop in the old way.’
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willoughby withdrew from deal of 11 million pounds
Peter Jones, one of the stars of Dragons Den, is 'furious' because TV presenter Holly Willoughby has withdrewed from an 11-million-pound deal with his lifestyle business. She wants to focus on her new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV
Willoughby doesn't have time for Truly, their brand for homewear and accessories.
Jones and Willoughby's company is sometimes compared to the Goop brand by Gwyneth Paltrow.
The 37-year-old presenter of This Morning announced on Instagram that she is leaving.
Peter Jones of Dragons Den is furious at Holly Willoughby, as she withdrewed from their profitable lifestyle business at the last minute to focus on her contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
According to sources, Jones was “furious” when Willoughby announced on Tuesday during a difficult meeting in the company’s headquarters in Marlow, Buckinghamshire that due to her new contracts (with a value of up to one and a half million pounds) she did not have enough time left for Truly, their brand for homewear and accessories.
The company is sometimes compared to Gwyneth Paltrow’s Goop brand and is expected to double Willoughby’s fortune of an estimated 11 million pounds.
When 37-year-old Willoughby announced on Instagram that she was leaving Truly, Jones left for one of his vacation rentals abroad.
“Truly was one of Holly’s top priorities.
It would ensure its long-term future, yielding enough for the next decades.
Everyone was stunned when she decided to retreat.
No one could believe their ears last Tuesday, because the launch was planned for soon.
The warehouse of the headquarters in Marlow is full of products that are ready for sale.’
Experts believe that the departure of the presenter, one of Britain's most profitable stars, could cost the company millions, as a result of the large investments in products from cushions and candles to clothing and homewear, and because the launch could now incur further delays.
And that could mean the end of years of friendship.
Willoughby and her husband Dan Baldwin, who have three children, have been good friends with Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby started Truly in 2016 together with Capp, and Jones, 52, became chairman of the company in March.
The two couples often go on holiday together and Jones has a 40 percent stake in Baldwin's television production company.
Willoughby will soon become brand ambassador of M&S and will replace Ant McPartlin as presenter of the program I'm A Celebrity on the British channel ITV.
A source from Jone’s entourage said last night, “We don’t comment on his business affairs.”
Tough language ‘and then it was love between us’
He joked about the criticism he would get in the media for a remark he made that some people would find “inappropriate for a president,” and for his positive view of the North Korean leader.
White has President Trump given up so much?
Trump imitated a news anchor when he said:
“I haven’t given up anything.”
He added that Kim is interested in a second meeting. Trump described their first meeting in June in Singapore a big step towards the dismantling of nuclear weapons in North Korea.
But negotiations on the removal of nuclear weapons have stalled.
More than three months after the summit in Singapore in June, top diplomat Ri Yong Ho of North Karey told leaders of the United Nations General Assembly that North Korea sees no "corresponding response" by the United States following North Korea's first disarmament initiatives.
The United States, meanwhile, is going ahead with its sanctions to keep pressure on the boiler, he says.
Trump was much more optimistic in his speech during the election rally.
"We're doing very well in North Korea," he said.
“We were close to a war with North Korea.
Millions of people would have died.
Now we have a fantastic relationship.”
He said his efforts to improve the relationship with Kim have led to positive results. The missile trials have ended, hostages have been liberated, and the remains of U.S
He also defended his unusual approach to his relationship with Kim.
'It's so easy to behave like a president, but instead of 10,000 people trying to gain access to these busy arena, about 200 people would be standing here,' Trump said, pointing to the public before him.
Tsunami and earthquake in Indonesia devastated an island, killing hundreds
In the aftermath of Lombok earthquake, for example, foreign NGOs were told that their assistance was not necessary.
Although more than 10% of Lombok's population no longer had a dwelling, no national disaster was declared, which is a requirement for launching international aid.
'In many cases, unfortunately, it's clear that they're not requesting international aid, which is difficult for us,' Ms Sumbung said.
Save the Children is putting together a team to travel to Palu, but it is not certain whether the rescuers from abroad can work on site.
Mr. Sutopo, spokesman for the National Disaster relief agency, said Indonesian officials were still evaluating the situation in Palu to see if international organizations would be allowed to help.
Although Indonesia is constantly plagued by earthquakes, the country is still particularly ill-prepared for the wrath of nature.
In Aceh shelters were built to hide from tsunamis, but along other coasts you rarely see them.
That there was no warning siren for tsunamis present in Palu, even though a warning was in effect, probably contributed to the number of deaths.
Even in ideal conditions it can be difficult to travel from one Indonesian island to another.
National disasters complicate the logistics even more.
A hospital ship docked in Lombok to treat earthquake casualties is now en route to Palu, but it will take at least three days for the ship to reach the scene of the disaster.
The improvement in Indonesia's ragged infrastructure was one of the cores of President Joko Widodo's election campaign, and he's already spent plenty of money on roads and railways.
But President Joko's government is contending with a shortage of funding, while he hopes to be re-elected next year.
President Joko is also pressured by persistent sectarian tensions in Indonesia, where members of the Muslim majority today follow a rather conservative form of their faith.
More than 1000 people were killed and tens of thousands had to leave their residences because Christian and Muslim gangs fight on the streets, with machetes, bow and arrow and other primitive weapons.
Look at: Daniel Sturridge of Liverpool makes for impressive equaliser against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool last Saturday from losing against Chelsea in the Premier League, with a goal in the 89th minute, during a match at Stamford Bridge in London.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri when he stood at a distance of about 30 yards from Chelsea's goal, at the time his squad stood 1-0 behind.
He tapped left against the ball and then kicked in the direction of the farthest pole.
The ball flew high above the penalty area, towards the top right corner of the net.
He dodged Kepa Arrizabalaga's jump and fell into the net.
“It was very difficult to reach that position, to reach the ball, and players like Shaq always play forward as much as possible,” Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
‘I saw Kante approaching and took a try. I didn’t really think about it, I just kicked on the ball.’
At half-time, Chelsea were 1-0 ahead, after a goal from the Belgian star Eden Hazard in the 25th minute.
The Blues striker kicked the ball with his heel to Mateo Kovacic, after which he rushed to Liverpool's half-to-play via midfield.
Kovacic did a quick one-twice in midfield.
Then he gave a nice sting pass, allowing Hazard to reach the penalty area.
Hazard was faster than the defence and kicked the ball with his left foot past Alisson Becker from Liverpool in the net, next to the back pole.
Jorplim plays Wednesday at 3pm in the group stages of the Champions League against Napoli, at Stadio San Paolo in Naples, Italy.
Chelsea will face Videoton in the UEFA Europa League on Thursday at 3 p.m
Death toll from tsunami in Indonesia rises to 832
The death toll from the earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia has risen to 832, the national disaster relief agency said Sunday morning.
Many people were trapped in the rubble of buildings that had collapsed as a result of the quake with a force of 7.5 from last Friday, which also created waves of 6 m high, spokesman Sutopo Purwo Nugroho said during a news conference.
The town of Palu, where more than 380,000 people live, is full of debris from collapsed buildings.
Polices arrest 32-year-old man on suspicion of murder after woman stabbed to death
This morning, a woman's body was found in Birkenhead in Merseyside, after which a murder investigation was opened.
The 44-year-old woman was found in Grayson Mews in John Street at 7:55 a.m
The police are asking the locals to come forward if they have seen or heard something.
Police Inspector Brian O'Hagan said: 'The investigation has only just begun, but I want to ask anyone who was near John Street in Birkenhead who has seen or heard anything suspicious to contact us.
I also want to ask everyone, especially taxi drivers, to get in touch if they may have filmed something via a dashcam. After all, they can have information that is essential for our investigation.'
A police spokesman confirmed that the woman whose body was finded is a local resident of Birkenhead and that she was found in a building.
This afternoon, friends who think they know the woman came to the scene to ask questions about where she was found this morning.
The investigation is ongoing and the police have confirmed that they are currently informing the victim's family.
A taxi driver living in Grayson Mews has tried to enter his apartment, but was told by the police that no one is allowed in or out of the building.
He was speechless when he discovered what happened.
Residents are now being told it's going to be hours before they can return home.
A policeman is said to have told someone that the area is being treated as a crime scene.
A woman appeared crying at the scene.
She keeps repeating: ‘How bad!’.
At 4 p.m
Within the deposition, a number of officers were keeping an eye on the apartment building.
Those who have information will be asked to send a message to @MerPolCC, to call 101 or to contact Crimestoppers anonymously on 0800 555 111, stating log 247 of 30 September.
Cromwell's statue to the British Parliament is newest victim of the 'rewriting history' riot
The banishment of the statue would be a poetic justice for the Taliban-like destruction of the many cultural and religious artifacts carried out by his fanatical Puritan followers.
But the Cromwell Society described Mr. Crick’s suggestion as “fooble” and “an attempt to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, said: “It was inevitable that the current debate about the removal of statues would lead to the targeting of Oliver Cromwell’s figure just outside the Houses of Parliament.
The iconoclasm of the English civil wars was neither commanded nor carried out by Cromwell.
Perhaps the wrong Cromwell would be sacrificed for the actions of his ancestor Thomas in the last century.
Cromwell's beautiful image, created by Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft, attests to the opinion that prevailed in the 19th century and is part of the historiography of a figure that many believe must still receive recognition today.
Mr. Goldsmith told The Sunday Telegraph: “Cromwell is regarded by many, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than today, as a defender of parliament against external pressure, in his case, of course, the monarchy.
Whether that is a completely correct performance, is still being debated.
What is certain, however, is that in the mid-17th century, the conflict has shaped the further development of our nation, and Cromwell is an individually recognizable figure representing one side of that gap.
His accomplishments as Lord Protector must also be acknowledged and commemorated.”
Murderous pig kills Chinese farmer
A farmer was attacked and killed by a pig at a market in south-west China, according to reports in the local media.
The man, of whom only the surname ‘Yuan’ is known, was found dead at a stable at the market in Liupanshui in Guizhou province. His artery was pierced and he was covered in blood, the South China Morning Post reported Sunday.
A pig farmer is preparing to vaccinate pigs at a pigstyle on May 30, 2005, in Xining in Qinghai, China province.
He is said to have traveled Wednesday with his cousin from neighbouring Yunnan province to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and turned out to be a door to a neighbouring pigstyle open.
According to the nephew, a large male pig with blood on his mouth was located in the pighouse.
A forensic examination found that the 250-kg pig had killed the farmer, the report said.
“My cousin’s legs were bloodied and disfigured,” the cousin said ‘Wu,’ according to Guiyang Evening News.
Footage from surveillance cameras shows that Yuan arrived at the market at 4:40 a.m. on Thursday to feed his pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man wasn't Yuan or his cousin.
A market manager told the Evening News that the pig had been locked up to prevent it from attacking others, while police gathered evidence on the spot.
Yuan's family and market authorities are reportedly currently negotiating compensation for his death.
It rarely happens, but there have been cases of pigs attacking people before.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband at their farm in Massachusetts, leaving the man seriously injured.
Ten years earlier, a 300-kg pig pushed a farmer from Wales against his tractor until his wife chased the animal away.
When an Oregon farmer was eaten by his pigs in 2012, a farmer from Manitoba at CBC News testified that pigs are not normally aggressive, but that the taste of blood can provoke aggression.
“They’re just playful.
They're like kids, very curious... they're not out to hurt people.
“You just have to show them the right amount of respect,” he said.
Hurricane Rosa's tail brings heavy rain to the south-west U.S
As predicted, Hurricane Rosa weakens now that she's reached the cooler waters of Mexico's north coast.
Rosa, however, will bring heavy rains to northern Mexico and the southwest in the coming days.
With a wind speed of 135 km per hour, Rosa was a Category 1 hurricane at 5 a.m. on Sunday morning, and was located 620 km from Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
Meanwhile, a trough begins to form over the Pacific Ocean, which is moving eastward to the west coast of the United States. As Rosa approaches the Baja California peninsula on Monday as a tropical storm, tropical humidity is pushed northward, to the southwest of the U.S.
Rosa will bring 10 inches of rain to parts of Mexico on Monday.
Subsequently, tropical moisture that responds with the approaching trough will cause widespread heavy raining in the southwest in the coming days.
Locally, 2 to 10 cm of rain will cause dangerous floods, debris flows and possibly landslides in the desert.
Tropical moisture will lead to precipitation from 5 to 8 cm per hour, especially in parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
As much as 5 to 10 cm of rain is expected in parts of the Southwest, especially over most of Arizona.
There are sudden floods possible with rapidly deteriorating conditions, as the tropical rain can fall very scattered.
It is strongly discouraged to go into the desert on foot when the tropical rainfall threatens.
The heavy rain can turn ravines into savagant rivers, and thunderstorms will produce local gusts and dust.
The approaching trough brings locally some heavy rain to parts of Southern California's coastline.
Rain of 1.5 cm is possible, which could lead to small debris flows and slippery roads.
This would be the region's first rainfall in the rainy season.
Scattered tropical rains will approach Arizona on Sunday evening until Monday morning, after which the rain will be more widely circulated on Monday at the end of the day and Tuesday.
Heavy rainfall will spread across the Four Corners on Tuesday and will continue to fall until Wednesday.
In October the temperature in the U.S. can fluctuate sharply, as the North Pole gets cooler, but the tropics remain quite warm.
This sometimes leads to large differences in temperature over short distances.
There's a fine example of impressive temperature differences in the middle of the U.S. on Sunday.
The temperature difference between Kansas City, Missouri, and Omaha, Nebraska, and between St. Louis and Des Moines, Iowa, is nearly 20 degrees.
In the next few days, the ongoing summer heat will try to build up and expand again.
In much of the middle and eastern U.S., October was expected to start warmly, with temperatures rocking around 30°C everywhere, from the southern plains to parts of the northeast.
In New York, it can turn 27 degrees on Tuesday, which is about 10 degrees warmer than the average.
Our long-term weather forecast shows that there is a high chance of above average temperatures in the Eastern United States until mid-October.
More than 20 million people were watching Brett Kavanaugh's hearing
More than 20 million people watched on one of the six television networks Thursday at Brett Kavanaugh's gripping testimony, candidate for U.S. Supreme Court, and Christine Blasey Ford, the woman accusing him of a sexual assault allegedly occurred in the 1980s.
Meanwhile, the political impasse continued, while broadcasters interrupted regular programming for Friday's surprise: an agreement arranged by Jeff Flake, senator from Arizona, to investigate the indictment for a week.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee that she's 100 percent sure a drunken Kavanaugh groped her and tried to take her clothes off during a high school party.
Kavanaugh stated in an animated testimony that he was 100 percent sure this hadn't happened.
Likely, more than the 20.4 million people reported by Nielsen watched on Friday.
The company counted the average number of viewers on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
There weren't immediately figures available for the other networks broadcasting the hearing, such as PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network.
And Nielsen usually has trouble counting people looking into an office.
By comparison, that's pretty much like for a play-off of a football match or for the Oscar ceremony.
Fox News Channel, which gives Kavanaugh's nomination all the support, had most viewers during the day of the hearing: averaging 5.69 million viewers, according to Nielsen.
ABC followed with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million viewers, NBC had 2.94 million viewers, MSNBC had 2.89 million viewers and CNN had 2.52 million viewers, according to Nielsen.
Even after the hearing, the public remained very interested.
Flake was the central figure of events on Friday.
After the moderate Republican's office issued a statement that he would support Kavanaugh with his vote, CNN and CBBs' cameras recorded on Friday morning that protesters were screaming at him as he tried to take the elevator to a Judiciary Committee hearing.
He stayed for a few minutes with their eyes knocked down as he was taunted, live on CNN.
"I'm standing right in front of you," said one woman.
“Do you think he’s telling the people the truth?
Someone shouted, ‘You have power, when so many women are powerless.’
Flake said his office had issued a statement, and closed right in front of the elevator, he added that he would say more during the hearing.
The various broadcasters sent everything out live when the judicial committee had to vote hours later on Kavanaugh's nomination in the Senate.
But Flake said he'd only vote in favor on the understanding that the FBI would investigate the allegations against the nominee by the following week, something minority Democrats insist on.
Flake was persuaded, among other things, by his friend Chris Coons, senator and Democrat.
Flake made his decision after a conversation with Coons and multiple senators.
Flake's choice was important, because it was clear that Republicans wouldn't have the necessary votes to approve Kavanaugh without the investigation.
President Trump has opened an FBI investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh.
British Prime Minister May accuses critics of 'a political game' surrounding Brexit
In an interview with the Sunday Times newspaper, Prime Minister Theresa May accused politicians who criticized her plans to leave the European Union from "a political game" with Britain's future and undermining national interests.
Prime Minister of Great Britain Theresa May arrives for the Conservative Party Conference in Birmingham, Great Britain, on September 29, 2018.
In another interview in addition, on the same front page of the newspaper, former Foreign Secretary Boris Johnson emphasized his attack on her so-called Chequers plan for Brexit. He said her proposal that Britain and the EU should collect each other’s levies was ‘absurd’.
Wayde Sims Shooting: Police arrest Dyteon Simpson as a suspect of LSU player's death
Police have arrested a suspect for the shooting in which Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player at LSU, was shot dead.
Dyteon Simpson, 20, has been arrested and is in prison on suspicion of manslaughter, Baton Rouge police said.
The authorities released a video of the confrontation between Sims and Simpson and the police said Sims lost his glasses during the fight.
The police found the glasses on the crime scene and found Simpson's DNA on the glasses, reports WAFB, a CBS television station.
Polices interrogated Simpson and later stated that he had confessed that he had shot Wayde with death as a result.
His deposit is set at 350,000 dollars, the Advocate reports.
The municipality's coroner in East Baton Rouge on Friday published a preliminary report stating that the cause of death is a gunshot wound to the neck.
The report states that the Louisiana State Police's Fugitive Task Force, state police crime lab, Southern University police and local residents have all contributed to the investigation that led to an arrest.
Sports director Joe Alleva of LSU thanked the local police for his "commitment and pursuit of justice."
Sims was 20 years old.
He was 198 cm tall and grew up in Baton Rouge, where his father Wayne also played basketball for LSU.
He scored an average of 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game last season.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade said the team is “broken” and “in shock” by Wayde’s death.
“This is an event that you always hope will never happen,” Wade says.
Volcano spews ashes on Mexico city
The ash being spewed out by the Popocatepetl volcano has reached the southern quarters of Mexico's capital.
The National Center for Disaster Prevention alerted Mexicans last Saturday to stay away from the volcano, after activity in the crater was picked up and 183 emissions of gas and ash had been recorded in a 24-hour period.
The center detected rumblings and vibrations several times.
On social media, photos of a thin layer of ash appeared on car windows in neighborhoods of Mexico city, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysics have observed an increase in activity in the volcano, which is located 72 kilometers southeast of the capital. That is the first time since September 2017, when an earthquake with a force of 7.1 on the Richter central Mexico scale struck.
The volcano, known as ‘Don Goyo’, has been active since 1994.
Police clash with Catalan separatists on the eve of the anniversary vote for independence
Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after pro-independence activists falled with rioting police, while thousands participated in rival demonstrations on the occasion of the first anniversary of the vote on independence in Catalonia that disintegrated the region into two groups.
A group of masked pro-separatists stopped by rioting police pelted the agents with eggs and powder paint, creating dark dust clouds in the streets usually filled with tourists.
Later in the day, skirmishes broke out with the police, and the officers pulled up their bat to stop the fighting.
For more than an hour, pro-independence groups sang: ‘Don’t forget, don’t forgive’, while their opponents shouted ‘Long live Spain’.
Fourteen people were being treated for minor injuries they sustained during the protests, the local press reported.
Tensions continue to rise high in the independence-minded region, a year after the October 1 referendum, which is considered illegal by Madrid but celebrated by separatist Catalans.
Voters overwhelmingly spoke out to become independent, although turnout was low and the people who oppose secession are boycotting the vote.
According to the Catalan authorities, almost 1000 people were injured last year after the police tried to stop the mood at the polling stations in the region, bringing it to violent skirmishes.
Pro-independence groups had camped at the scene the night from Friday to Saturday to prevent a demonstration in support of national police.
The demonstration continued, but they were forced to take a different route.
Narcissus Termes, 68, an electrician attending the separatist protest along with his wife, said he was no longer hopeful about Catalonia's prospects for becoming independent.
“Last year we experienced one of our most beautiful moments.
I saw how my parents cried with joy because they could vote, but now we're stuck,' he said.
Although the Catalan pro-independence parties achieved an essential victory in the regional elections in December, even though it was nifty, it was difficult for them this year to build on their success. After all, many of their best-known leaders are absent, either in self-imposed exile or in captivity pending a trial over their role in organizing the referendum and exclamation of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old technician who filmed the protest in support of the police on his phone, said the conflict had been stoked by politicians on both sides.
"Tensions are increasing," he said.
Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders who has been in pre-trial detention since late last year, declared that he will take part in the European parliamentary elections next year.
“A candidacy for the European elections is the best way to denounce the deterioration of the democratic values and repression we have seen of the Spanish government,” he said.
Londonderry: men arrested after house was rammed with a car
Three men, aged 33, 34 and 39, have been arrested after a car was repeatedly rammed into a London-derry house.
The incident occurred in Ballynagard Crescent around 19:30 on Thursday.
Police Inspector Bob Blemmings said the gates and the building themselves had sustained damage.
Possibly at some point a crossbow was fired at the car.
Livingston wins 1-0 against Rangers thanks to Menga's goal
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston gave them victory
The promoted Livingston surprised the Rangers and delivered Steven Gerrard his second defeat in a series of 18 games since he became manager of the Ibrox club.
Dolly Menga's goal made the difference and Gary Holt's squad equalised with Hibernian.
Gerrard's team failed to win an away game this season during the Premiership. They have to take it Sunday against leader Hearts, which has eight points ahead of them.
But first the Rangers receive Rapid Vienna in the Europa League on Thursday.
Livingston, meanwhile, remains undefeated. The team has already won six games consecutively in his department, and coach Holt has not suffered a single defeat since he had Kenny Miler replaced last month.
Livingston misses opportunities against strong visitors
Holt's team could have achieved a lead long before the first goal. After all, their direct way of playing caused all kinds of problems with the Rangers.
Scott Robinson broke through but failed to take it off with a goal. Subsequently, Alan Lithgow, who caught Craig Halkett's header, kicked the ball above goal.
The home team let the Rangers play with great pleasure, and awaited its chances calmly and confidently.
And that ultimately led to the crucial goal.
The Rangers allowed a free kick and Livingston ensured an opening. Declan Gallagher and Robinson worked together to create an opportunity for Menga, who scored from the penalty area.
At the time, the Rangers had had possession for quite some time, but the home team's defence was adamant and goalkeeper Liam Kelly hadn't had to do much yet.
That pattern continued in the second half, although Alfredo Morelos ensured that Kelly had to avoid a goal.
Scott Pittman kicked the ball against the feet of Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor and Lithgow missed the goal after a pass from his Livingston colleagues.
Continuously, passes reached Livingston's pencillary area, but they were banned each time. Twice a penalty kick was denied, one after Halkett's attack on substitute Glenn Middleton and one for handball.
'Phenomenal' by Livingston - analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland at the Tony Macaroni Arena
It's a phenomenal performance and result for Livingston.
They played without exception excellent and continue to exceed expectations in their race to the top.
Their style of play and the composition of their team has barely changed since their return to the highest regions, but especially Holt deserves a lot of recognition for the way he has whipped up the team since his arrival.
He had so many heroes.
Captain Halkett was fantastic, with an excellently organized defence, while Menga Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall had been walking on the tops of their toes throughout the match.
The Rangers, however, did not manage to break through.
Even though they have often played very well under Gerrard, this time they did not reach their usual level.
Their final phase was not good enough (they only managed to outplay the home team once) and the Rangers will now realize that they have to play better. After all, they are somewhere in the middle of the standings.
Erdogan is received with mixed feelings in Cologne
Under a shining blue sky and with a broad smile, the leaders of Turkey and Germany went for breakfast together in Berlin on Saturday (September 29).
It was the last day of President Erdogan's controversial visit to Germany, which is aimed at restoring relations between NATO allies.
They disagree on issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey's accession to the EU.
Erdogan then left for Cologne for the opening of a giant new mosque.
Cologne is home to the largest Turkish population outside Turkey.
Polices invoked security reasons as she prevented the crowd of 25,000 people from gathering in front of the mosque, but many sympathizers came close to seeing their president.
Hundreds of protesters, many of them Kurds, also made their voices heard. They condemned both Erdogan's policies and German government's decision to welcome him to the country.
The rival protests reflect the division over a visitor praised by some German Turks as a hero and accused by others as an autocrate.
Traffic accident in Deptford: cyclist dies after car collision
In London, a cyclist was killed in a collision involving a car.
The accident happened near the intersection of Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street, a busy road in Deptford, in southeast the city, at about 10.15pm local time.
The driver of the car stopped and the ambulance crews were present, but the man died at the scene.
A few months ago, another cyclist died in a hit-and-run accident on Children's Street, about one and a half kilometers from Saturday's accident.
The Metropolitan Police said agents were in the process of identifying the man and will notify his immediate family.
The road is closed in different places, buses are diverted and motorists are advised to avoid the area.
Loung Lartin's Prison: Six jailers injured in order disturbance
Six jailers have been injured in an order disturbance in a high-security prison for men, according to the prison office.
The unrest broke out Sunday at about 9.30 a.m. local time at HMP Long Lartin in Worcestershire and is still ongoing.
Specialized ‘Tornado’ jailers have been deployed to resolve situation involving eight prisoners in one department.
The jailers were treated there for minor injuries to the face.
“Specially trained prison staff have been deployed for a current incident in HMP Long Lartin.
Six staff have been treated for injuries.
We do not tolerate violence in our prisons and those responsible are at least referred to the police and may have to stay behind bars longer.’
HMP Long Lartin captures more than 500, including some of the country's most dangerous criminals.
In June, it was reported that the director of the prison had been treated in hospital after being attacked by an inmate.
And in October last year, riot police were called to prison for a serious disturbance in which staff were attacked with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas, Salt Lake City with sudden floods (with potentially positive consequences for drought areas)
It rarely happens that a tropical depression reaches Arizona, but that’s exactly what is likely to happen early next week. That’s because Hurricane Rosa’s remaining energy is moving across the southwest of the desert area, which entails risks to flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued warnings of sudden flooding for west of Arizona to the south and east of Nevada for Monday and Tuesday, and southeastern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to hang just above Phoenix on Tuesday. Monday night it is already going to rain.
The National Weather Service in Phoenix noted in a tweet that “since 1950, only ten tropical cyclones have maintained the status of tropical storm or depression in the 200-mile area!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane within 40 miles of the Arizona border.”
The most recent models of the National Hurricane Center predict 5 to 10 inches of precipitation, with insulated quantities up to 15 centimeters in Arizona's Mogollon Rim.
Other parts of the southwest of the desert region, including the central Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin, are likely to get 3 to 5 cm of rain, and 10 cm is possible here and there.
For the areas that are not at risk of flooding, Rosa's rain can be a blessing, as the region is affected by drought.
Although floods are very worrying, part of this rainfall is likely to be beneficial, as the Southwest is currently experiencing a drought.
According to the U.S. Drought Monitor, the map on which current drought areas are indicated, slightly more than 40 percent of Arizona is experiencing at least extreme drought, the second-the-highest category,' weather.com reported.
Hurricane Rosa's trajectory reaches the mainland through Mexico's Baja California peninsula.
Rosa is still at hurricane strength on Sunday morning with a maximum wind speed of 140 km per hour, is 620 km south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico and is moving northward at a speed of 20 km per hour.
The storm strikes cooler waters in the Pacific Ocean, weakening them.
That's why she's expected to arrive in Mexico on Monday in the afternoon or evening with the force of a tropical storm.
Severe rain may fall in parts of Mexico, which entails a significant flood risk.
‘Precipitation of a total of 8 to 15 cm is expected from Baja California to the northwest of Sonora, with up to 25 cm possible,’ reported weather.com.
Rosa is moving north across Mexico as a tropical storm before reaching the border with Arizona on Tuesday in the early morning hours as a tropical depression, which will then move through Arizona late on Tuesday night and in southern Utah.
“The greatest danger expected of Rosa or her tail is very heavy rainfall in Baja California, northwest Sonora, and the south-west of the desert region,” the National Hurricane Center said.
This rain is expected to lead to life-threatening sudden floods and debris flows in the deserts and landslides on mountainous terrain.
Midsomer Norton attack: four arrests on suspicion of attempted murder
Three teenage boys and a 20-year-old man have been arrested on suspicion of attempted murder after a 16-year-old with stab wounds was found in Somerset.
The teen was found injured at about 4 a.m. local time on Saturday in the Excelsior Terrace district of Midsomer Norton.
He was taken to hospital and his condition is "stable."
A 17-year-old, two 18-year-old and a 20-year-old man were arrested overnight in the Radstock area, Avon and Somerset police said.
The police are asking anyone who may have filmed something of what has happened to come forward.
Trump says Kavanaugh 'has suffered from the Democratic Party's meanness, anger'
'A vote for Judge Kavanaugh is a vote to reject the Democratic Party's relentless and outrageous tactics,' Trump said during a rally in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump said Kavanaugh, during his appointment procedure, has constantly "suffered under the meanness, anger" of the Democratic Party
Kavanaugh testified in front of Congress on Thursday and vigorously and emotionally denied Christine Blasey Ford's allegation that he sexually assaulted her decades ago when they were teenagers.
Ford also testificated about her allegation during the hearing.
The president said on Saturday that Kavanaugh's "U.S. people saw "the dazzling and incredible character, quality and courage."
“A vote to recognize Judge Kavanaugh is a voice that recognizes one of the most accomplished legal opinions of our time, a lawyer with an excellent track record,” he told the crowd advocates in West Virginia.
The president was indirectly referring to Kavanaugh's appointment when he spoke about the importance of Republican turnout in the midterm elections.
“One of the most important elections in our lives will take place in five weeks.
I don't participate, but actually I do participate,' he said.
‘That’s why I stand up everywhere for great candidates.’
Trump stated that the Democrats are on a mission to "resist and hinder us."
The first key procedural vote in the Senate on Kavanaugh's nomination is expected to take place no later than Friday, a top GOP leadership employee told CNN.
Hundreds killed by Indonesian earthquake, tsunami, and the toll is getting higher
In a major earthquake and tsunami on the Indonesian island Sulawesi, at least 384 people were killed, wiping many off the ground when giant waves crashed on the beach
Hundreds of people had gathered Friday for a festival on the beach in the town of Palu, when waves of up to six meters high struck ashore the moment it began to twilight. Many people crashed and the waves destroyed everything in their path.
The tsunami was the result of an earthquake with a force of 7.5 on the Richter scale.
‘When the tsunami threat arose yesterday, there were still people on the beach. They didn’t immediately walk away and thus fell victim to the tsunami,’ said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the Indonesian agency for help with natural disasters BNPB during a briefing in Jakarta.
'The tsunami also led to other dangers: he towed cars, tree trunks, houses, he hit everything on land,' said Nugroho, who added that the tsunami had traveled over the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before he reached the coastline.
Some people climbed a tree to escape the tsunami and survived, he said.
About 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs released by the agency for help in natural disasters show that many buildings and shops have been destroyed, bridges twisted and collapsed and a mosque is surrounded by water.
On Saturday, the coastal city continued to tremble by aftershocks.
The series of earthquakes was felt in an area where 2.4 million people live.
The Indonesian Agency for the Assessment and Application of Technology (the BPPT) said in a statement that the energy released in Friday's massive earthquake was about 200 times the strength of the atomic bomb that had fallen on Hiroshima in World War II.
It's possible that the city's geography, which lies at the end of a long, narrow bay, still enlarged the tsunami, he said.
Nugroho described the damage as 'significant' and said that thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
Some fatalities were trapped under the rubble of collapsed buildings, he said. Some 540 people were injured and 29 went missing.
Nugroho said the number of casualties and damage could be even greater along the coastline 300 km north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, which is closer to the earthquake's epicentre.
The communication with Donggala ‘is completely flat, we don’t have any information at all’, Nugroho said.
More than 300,000 people live there, the Red Cross said in a statement. The staff and volunteers of the Red Cross are on their way to the affected areas.
“This is already a tragedy, but it can get much worse,” the Red Cross said.
The organization received a lot of criticism on Saturday because she had not said a tsunami had hit Palu, although employees said the waves had reached the country within the time the warning had been given.
In amateur images shared on social media, it can be heard how a man on the upper floor of a building is screaminging at people on the street downstairs to warn them that a tsunami is coming.
Minutes later, a wall of water crashes onto the coast and buildings and cars are dragged along.
Reuters could not immediately verify the footage.
The earthquake and tsunami caused a major power outage that cut off communications around Palu, making it difficult for authorities to coordinate rescue efforts.
The military is currently sending cargo planes with relief supplies from Jakarta and other cities, authorities said, but the evacués are still in dire need for food and other basic needs.
The city's airport has been reopened only for assistance and will remain closed to the public until October.
President Joko Widodo will visit evacuation centres in Palu on Sunday.
Death toll from tsunami rises above 800.
- It's very bad.
World Visision employees from Donggala may have arrived safely in the city of Palu, where workers in the courtyard of their office seek shelter in tarpaulin tents, but passed scenes of devastation along the way, Doseba said.
"They told me they saw a lot of houses that had been destroyed," he said.
- It's very bad.
Even when aid groups started with the difficult task of launching emergency aid, some complained that certain foreign aid workers with great expertise could not travel to Palu.
According to Indonesian regulations, funding, goods and personnel from abroad can only be allowed if the location of a disaster is proclaimed as a national natural disaster area.
- That hasn't happened yet.
"It's still a disaster at the province level," said Aulia Arriani, a spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
‘As soon as the government says, ‘O.K., this is a national disaster’, we can provide international assistance, but that status has still not been declared.’
When the second night after Friday's earthquake and tsunami fell on Palu, friends and family of the missing continued to hope that they would find their loved ones' lives, as in the miracles that people love to hear in terrible natural disasters like this.
On Saturday, a boy was taken out of a sewer.
On Sunday, aid workers freed a woman who had been trapped under the rubble for two days with her mother's corpse beside her.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of the Indonesian national paragliding team, had trained two of the missing paragliders for the Asian Games, which had taken place in Indonesia earlier this month.
His students were also stuck at the Roa Roa Hotel, including Mr. Mandagi.
‘As a veteran in the paragliding world, I carry my own emotional burden,’ he said.
Mr. Gendon recounted that the news that the Roa Roa Hotel had collapsed had spread in the paragliding community and that in the hours after that he had desperately sent WhatsApp messages to the athletes in Palu, who participated in the beach festival there.
With his messages, however, only one gray checkmark appeared instead of two blue checkmarks.
'I think that means the messages haven't been delivered,' he said.
Thieves steal 26,750 dollars while supplying ATM in Newport on the Levee
Friday morning, thieves stole 26,750 dollars from a Brink employee who was filling an ATM in Newport on the Levee, according to a Newport police press release.
The driver had just emptied an ATM in the entertainment complex and wanted to fill him with money, Inspector Dennis McCarthy wrote.
When he's at it, another man walked from behind Brink's collaborator and stole a bag of money that was ready to deliver.
Witnessees saw multiple suspects fleeing, according to the press release, but police did not say how many persons were involved in the incident.
If you have information about the identity of the perpetrators, you will be asked to contact the Newport police on the number 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: Rapper changes his name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West changes his name to Ye.
Last Saturday he announced the change on Twitter with the words: 'The creature formally known as Kanye West'.
The 41-year-old West has been briefly called Ye for a while and used this nickname as the title for his eighth album, which came out in June.
The name change was announced in the run-up to his performance on Saturday Night Live, where he is expected to present his new album Yandhi.
He replaces singer Ariana Grande, who canceled her performance during the show for "emotional reasons," says the show's creator.
Ye is not only an abbreviation of his current stage name. Earlier, West already told that the word has a religious meaning for him.
I think ‘ye’ is the most common word in the Bible, and in the Bible it means ‘you’, West said earlier this year, when he talked about the title of his album with radio presenter Big Boy.
“So I’m you, I’m us, it’s us.
I’ve switched from Kanye, which means ‘the only one’, to ordinary Ye, a reflection of the good in us, the bad, the confused, everything.
Rather, the album is a reflection of who we are.”
He's not the only famous rapper to change his name.
Sean Combs was formerly known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but announced this year's preference for the names Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, who used to work with West, has already worked with and without the hyphen and capital letters.
Mexican president vows not to use armed forces against civilians
Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador, Mexico's president-elect, has pledged never to use military power against civilians. The country stands on the eve of the 50th anniversary of a bloody reprisal against students.
Lopez Obrador pledged at Tlatelolco Plaza on Saturday that he “would never use military force to oppress the Mexican people.”
On October 2, 1968, military troops fired at a peaceful demonstration in the square, killing as many as 300 people. During that period, left-wing student movements occurred throughout Latin America.
Lopez Obrador has pledged to help young Mexicans by giving monthly grants to students and opening more free public universities.
He said that young people often join criminal gangs due to unemployment and a shortage of opportunities to study.
United States must double A.I. funding
As China becomes more active in the field of artificial intelligence, the United States has to double the amount they spend on research by A.I., says investor and A.I. specialist Kai-Fu Lee, who has worked for already Google, Microsoft and Apple.
He says this in response to the announcements about A.I. that come from various departments of the U.S. government, despite the fact that the U.S. does not have a formal strategy for A.I.
In the meantime, China announced its plan last year: the country wants to be the number 1 in terms of A.I. innovations by 2030.
“Doubling the budget for research into A.I. would already be a good start, given that all the other countries are so far behind the U.S., and we’re looking for the next breakthrough in A.I.”
Doubling funding could lead to a doubling of the likelihood that the next major achievement for A.I. in the U.S. is taking place, Lee said in an interview with CNBC this week.
Lee, whose book ‘AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order’ was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in Face++, one of China’s major A.I. companies.
In the 1980's he worked at Carnegie Mellon University on an A.I. system that defeated American Othello's best player, and later he was manager at Microsoft Research and President of Google's Chinese department.
Lee expressed his appreciation for technology competitions organized by the U.S. government, such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency's Robotics Challenge, and asked when the next competition would take place, as it would help to discover the following visionaries.
In the U.S
‘China doesn’t steal our best academics, the companies do that,» Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have recruited the best forces of universities to work on A.I.
Lee said that U.S. efforts on A.I. would also take advantage of an immigration policy change.
“Those who are a PhD in the field of A.I. should automatically be offered a green card,” he said.
The Chinese National Council released its development plan for next-generation artificial intelligence in July 2017.
The Chinese Foundation for Natural Sciences provides financial support to employees of academic institutions, just as the National Science Foundation and other government organizations give money to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work is lower in China, Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense set up a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, intended to involve partners from industry and academia in the initiative, and the White House announced the formation of the Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
And this month, DARPA announced that 2 billion dollars would be invested in the AI Next initiative.
The NSF is currently investing more than 100 million dollars in research on A.I.
The U.S. legislation that should lead to the creation of a National Artificial Intelligence Security Commission has been in the slump for months, however.
Macedonians vote in referendum on possible name change from their country
Last Sunday, the Macedonian people voted in a referendum on the possible change of their country’s name in ‘Republic of North Macedonia’. With this, a dispute with Greece that has been dragging on for decades would be resolved and Greece would no longer impede the Macedonian application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a province also called Macedonia, and says its northern neighbor's name implies that the country is claiming on Greek territory. Consequently, Greece vetoed the inclusion of Macedonia in the European Union and NATO.
The two governments concluded an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalists feel that the name change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority in Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he is not going to vote in the referendum and due to a boycott campaign it is not certain that the required turnout of at least 50 percent will be achieved, so the referendum may not be valid.
“Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU with acceptance of the agreement with Greece?”
‘Proponents of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, feel that the price that has to be paid is worth trying to join bodies such as the EU and NATO. Macedonia is one of the countries that originated when the former Yugoslavia disintegrated.
“I come today to vote for the future of the country, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because that will lead to a safer life for all of us, said 79-year-old Olivera Georgijevska in Skopje.
Although the referendum is not legally binding, sufficient MPs have indicated that they will respect the results, making them decisive.
For the name change, a two-thirds majority in parliament would be required.
The National Electoral Commission said there had been no reports of irregularities by 13 a.m.
Turnout, however, was only 16 percent at the time, compared with 34 percent during the previous parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters came to vote.
‘I come to vote because it is important for my children. Our place is in Europe, said 62-year-old Gjose Tanevski, a voter in the capital Skopje.
Premier Zoran Zaev of Macedonia, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko voted in Strumica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018, during the referendum around the name change of the country that would open the way for membership of NATO and the European Union.
In front of parliament in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov set up a small stage and placed chairs in front of tents that had been placed by people boycotting the referendum.
“We are in favour of NATO and the EU, but we want to participate as a full member, and not through a loft gate,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have our dignity.
If they do not want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to other countries, such as China and Russia, and become part of European-Asian integration."
PM Zaev says NATO's membership will deliver much-needed investment for Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of more than 20 percent.
'I am convinced that the vast majority are in favour, because more than 80 percent of our citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO,' Zaev said after casting his vote.
He said that a ‘yes’ as a result would be ‘a confirmation of our future’.
A poll published last Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Policy Research found that between 30 and 43 percent of voters were allowed to take part in the referendum, less than the required turnout.
From another poll, conducted by hot Macedonian TV station Telma TV, it was concluded that 57 percent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 percent would vote yes.
If the referendum is to be successful, the turnout must be 50 percent plus one vote.
A failure of the referendum would be the first serious blow to pro-Western government's policy since it came to power in May last year.
Look at: Sergio Aguero of Manchester City passes full defence of Brighton and scores
Last Saturday, Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling made short with Brighton's defence at the Etihad stage in Manchester, England. Manchester City won the match 2-0.
It seemed like a penny whistle, how Aguero scored in the 65th minute.
The Argentine striker first got the ball played in midfield.
He raced between three Brighton defenders, straight into the open field.
Aguero was surrounded by four green shirts.
He managed to pass a defender and was also too quick for a few others on the edge of Brighton's penalty area.
Then he gave a pass to the left, where Sterling was waiting.
The English forward used his first action in the penalty area to return the ball to Aguero, who used his right shoe to beat goalkeeper Mathew Ryan with a shot in the right side of the net.
‘Aguero suffers from problems with his feet,» Manchester City coach Pep Guardiola told the press.
“We decided to let him play for 55 to 60 minutes.
And that's happened, too.
We were lucky that he scored a goal at the time.”
But it was Sterling who gave the Sky Blues the first advantage in the Premier League.
That goal came in the 29th minute.
Aguero got the ball when he stood deep in Brighton's playing field.
He sent a beautiful sting pass along the left flank to Leroy Sane.
Sane kept the ball with him for a moment and then sent Sterling to the second pole.
The Sky Blues player tapped the ball into the net, just before he was too far.
City will take on Hoffenheim at 12:55 a.m. on Tuesday in the group stage of the Champions League. The match will take place in the Rhein-Neckar-Arena in Sinsheim, Germany.
Scherzer wants to play along and don't make it easy for the Rockies to reach the playoff
Now that the Washington Nationals are eliminated for the play-off, there wasn't much reason to force a fresh start.
Still, the ever-competitive Scherzer hopes to play Sunday against the Colorado Rockies, but only if there are still consequences for the participation of the Rockies in the play-off. They are currently one game ahead of the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NL West.
The Rockies have secured at least a wild card for the play-off thanks to their 5-2 win against the Nationals last Friday, but still want to make every effort now to secure their first department title.
“Although there’s nothing left for us, at least we can pitch with the knowledge that the atmosphere here in Denver with this audience will be optimal and the other team is probably playing at the highest level I’d face this year.
Why wouldn’t I want to play in it?”
The Nationals have yet to announce who will be on the move on Sunday, but are reportedly inclined to draft Scherzer as a pitcher in such a situation.
Scherzer, who would make his 34th start, was in the throw-in field on Thursday and would still pitch next Sunday, although that's normally his rest day.
Washington's right-handed pitcher stands at 18-7 with an ERA of 2.53 and 300 strike-outs in 220 2/3 innings this season.
