Labour is no longer a broad church.
The party is intolerant to people who say what they think
When Momentum's activists voted in my local party to censore me, that wasn't a surprise.
After all, I'm the latest in a whole series of Labor MPs who got that we're not welcome just because we say what we're thinking.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan received a similar treatment because she resolutely came up against anti-Semitism.
In my case, I was penalized with the censorship motion because I didn't agree with Jeremy Corbyn.
About the importance of responsible economic policy, about national security, about Europe, ironically similar issues such as these in which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
In the announcement of the Nottingham East Labour meeting on Friday it was stated: ‘we want the meetings to be inclusive and productive.’
And that was also the case during most GC meetings on Friday evening during the eight years that I am already a local Labour MP.
Unfortunately, that's not the tone of many meetings today, and the promise of a "nicer, softer" politics has long since been forgotten, if she'd ever started.
It is becoming increasingly clear that a dissenting opinion will not be tolerated in the Labour party and any opinion will be judged on whether it is acceptable for the party leadership or not.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader. Colleagues who, as far as I knew, had a similar political outlook as me, suddenly expected that I would make a turnaround and take a position that I would otherwise never have agreed to, from topics such as national security to the European single market.
Every time I speak in front of an audience, no matter what I say, there are abuses on social media calling for my candidacy as an MP for the next election, denouncing the politics of the centre and I am told that I should not be a Labour member.
And that's only what I'm going through.
Indeed, I know I'm more lucky than some of my colleagues, because the remarks to my address are generally political.
I'm impressed by the professionalism and determination of those colleagues dealing every day with a torrent of sexist or racist responses, but don't get intimidated.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this era of politics is that bad abuses are apparently considered normal.
Jeremy Corbyn claimed last week that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer that broad church and with every ‘motion of distrust’ or change of selection rules the party becomes smaller.
Over the past two years, I’ve often been advised to lay low, not just loudly express my opinion, and then I ‘don’t get into trouble’.
But that's why I didn't go into politics.
Since I joined Labour 32 years ago as a schoolchild, provoked by the neglect of Thatcher's government through which my classroom literally collapsed, I have always advocated for better public services for those who need them most, both in my role as a city councillor and in my role as minister.
I've never concealed my politics, even in the last election.
Everyone in Nottingham East had clarity on my policy positions and my points of contention with the current leadership.
To those who propagated the motion on Friday, I can only say one thing: when the country drags itself towards a Brexit that will harm families, businesses and our public services, I don't understand the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the leader of the Labour Party.
But actually I have only one message, and it is not addressed to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, whether they are members of Labour or not: I am proud to be able to serve you and I promise that threats to reject my candidacy as an employment for the next election or political opportunities will not prevent me from acting according to what I think is in your interest.
Chris Leslie is MP for Nottingham East
Ayr 38 - 17 Melrose: undefeated Ayr on his way to the top
Two late try’s may have the end result somewhat crooked, but there’s no doubt Ayr rightfully triumphs in this delightful match of the day in the Tennent’s Premiership.
They are now at the top of the standings, the only undefeated team out of ten.
In the end, it was both their superior defence and their better chances that gave the home team the victory, and coach Peter Murchie was allowed to be satisfied.
'We've had a hard time so far during our games and we're still undefeated, so I'm a lucky man,' he said.
Robyn Christie van Melrose: ‘All credit to Ayr, they used their chances better than us.’
Grant Anderson's try in the 14th minute, finished by Frazier Climo, led to a lead for Ayr, but through a yellow card by Rory Hughes of the Scottish national squad (which he got during the Warriors' match) Melrose was able to let the numbers speak and Jason Baggot managed to break down a try.
Climo provided an extra lead for Ayr with a penalty and scored a conversion after a solotry just before halftime. That's how it became 17-5 for Ayr after the first half.
But Melrose started the second half well and Patrick Anderson's try, followed by Baggot's conversation, reduced the lead to five points.
The match lay still for a long time for a serious injury by Ruaridh Knott, who was drained on a stretcher, and once the match had restarted, Ayr could walk further through a try from Stafford McDowall followed by a Climo conversion.
Then captain Blair Macpherson of Ayr got a yellow card, and again Melrose reckoned with the extra man when no conversion followed a try by Bruce Colvine at the end of a match where the pressure was very high.
The home team came back, however, and when Struan Hutchinson was given a yellow card because he tackled Climo without the ball, MacPherson behind the advancing Ayr-maul provided a touch-down to the penalty-line-out.
Climo took care of the conversion, and did so again almost immediately after the restart, after Kyle Rowe caught David Armstrong's box kick and half-back Gregor sent Henry away for the home team's fifth try.
Star of Still Game is ready for new career in the restaurant world
It looks like Actor Ford Kieran from Still Game is going into the hospitality industry. Apparently, he has been appointed director of a restaurant business.
The 56-year-old plays Jack Jarvis in the popular BBC series, which he writes and in which he himself acts along with his comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The duo has announced that the upcoming ninth series will be the last in the series, and it appears that Kiernan is already planning his life after Craiglang.
According to official records, he's Adriftmorn Limited director.
The actor didn't want to comment on the story, but a source from the Scottish Sun hinted that Kiernan was looking for a way to participate in Glasgow's 'thriving restaurant business'.
'The sea belongs to us': Landlocked Bolivia hopes the court will reopen the path to the Pacific
Sailors patrolling a rigged naval office in La Paz.
An ocean-blue flag is waving on public buildings.
On all naval bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon is the motto: ‘The sea belongs to us.
It’s a duty to reclaim them.”
Surrounded by land Bolivia, the memory of the coastland the country has had to cede to Chile after a bloody nineteenth-century commodity conflict is still vivid, as is the desire to sail the Pacific Ocean again.
That hope may well have reached its highest point in recent decades, as Bolivia awaits a ruling by the International Court of Justice, which should fall on October 1 after five years of deliberation.
“Bolivia has the momentum, a spirit of unity and serenity, and, of course, looks forward to the result with a positive outlook,” said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians across the country will look at the Court's verdict on big screens, in the hope that the tribunal in The Hague will argue in favour of Bolivia that Chile, after decades of feisty talks, is obliged to give Bolivia a sovereign access to the sea.
Also for Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president, who faces a controversial battle for re-election next year, much is at stake with Monday's verdict.
“We are very close to returning to the Pacific,” he swore at the end of August.
But some analysts believe that the court probably won't decide in Bolivia's favor, and even if it did, little would change.
The U.N. body based in the Netherlands does not have the power to allocate Chilean territory and has indicated that it will not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the ruling of the International Court of Justice falls just six months after the closing arguments indicates that the case was ‘not complicated’, says Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
And the prospects for a positive result from Bolivia have certainly not improved, on the contrary, over the past four years the situation has perhaps gotten even worse.
‘The case surrounding access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian government,’ Zárate said.
Morales' warlike rhetoric has completely undermined all the goodwill that Chile might have left, she claimed.
Bolivia and Chile will continue their talks at some point, but it will be extremely difficult to continue talking after this.
The two countries do not have an ambassador to each other's countries since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, representing Bolivia in The Hague, denies that the court's decision was taken unusually quickly.
On Monday, Bolivia "will have an extraordinary opportunity to start a new era of international relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "end 139 years of disagreements, with mutual benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who is still one of Latin America's most most-most-most presidents, uses the maritime issue as a politary stool.
"Bolivia will never give up his right of access to the Pacific," he added.
“The verdict is an opportunity to see that we need to put the past behind us.”
North Korea says nuclear disarmament won't be possible until the U.S
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho says his country will never remove its nuclear weapons if it can't trust Washington.
Ri spoke on Saturday at the United Nations General Assembly.
He called on the United States to deliver on the promises that were made at a summit among the leaders of the rivals in Singapore.
He said this at the moment that Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be about to restart stalled nuclear diplomacy, more than three months after the summit in Singapore with Kim Jong Un of North Korea.
Ri says it's a 'utopia' that the enduring sanctions and the U.S. objection to a statement ending the Korean War will someday get North Korea to its knees.
Washington is not inclined to agree with the statement without Pyongyang first taking important steps on disarmament.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
But there is skepticism prevailing and there is doubt that Pyongyang is serious when he says he would refrain from an arsenal that the country probably sees as its only way to guarantee its safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit between Kim and Trump.
Fashion shows in Paris unveil the latest collection of huge headgears, soon to be on sale near you
If you want to expand your hat collection or keep the sun completely out of your eyes, look no further.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne unveiled a series of crazy oversized headgear for their SS19 collection on the catwalk, a striking move during Paris Fashion Week.
Very impractical hats were unthinkable of Instagram this summer and these designers sent their dazzling creations onto the catwalk.
Valentino's striking piece was a giant beige hat decorated with a feather-like wide edge, which hung up to the eyes of the models.
Other oversized accessories included watermelons set with gemstones, a wizard's hat, and even a pineapple, but those aren't designed to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also unveiled a selection of bizarre masks, just in time for Halloween.
With many of the colorful masks, the lips were sewn shut. They looked more like Hannibal Lecter than haute couture.
One creation resembled diving equipment, complete with snorkeling and glasses, while another resembled a melted ice cream.
And if you want to follow this daring fashion, you're lucky.
Style watchers predict that the huge hats will find their way to the clothing stores near you.
The giant hats may well be inspired by 'La Bomba', the straw hat with a two-meter-wide edge that everyone wears, from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult label behind the highly impractical hat that surfaced everywhere on social media also resulted in another big creation on the catwalk: a straw beach bag that was almost as big as the model in swimsuit carrying the bag.
The red orange raffia bag, trimmed with raffia frills and finished with a white leather handle, was the most striking piece in Jacquemus's La Riviera SS19 collection during Paris Fashion Week.
Famous stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: '⁁I expect to see big hats and beach bags lying in the shops next summer. Since the designer has made such a huge impact, it would be difficult to ignore the demand for extra-large accessories."
John Edward: Language skills essential for global citizens
The independent schools in Scotland have an excellent track record of academic excellence, and this continued in 2018 with again a series of excellent exam results, and moreover individual and collective success in sports, arts, music and other initiatives of the entire community.
With more than 30,000 pupils in Scotland, these schools, represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), aim to provide the best service to their pupils and parents.
Independent schools strive to prepare their pupils for secondary and higher education, their chosen careers and their place as global citizens.
As an educational sector that can design and implement a bespoke school curriculum, we see that modern languages remain popular and desired subjects par excellence within schools.
Nelson Mandela said: ‘If you talk to someone in a language he understands, you reach his head.
“When you talk to him in his own language, you reach his heart.”
This is a powerful reminder that we can't rely solely on English when we want to build relationships and a relationship of trust with people from other countries.
From this year's recent exam results, we can infer that languages are at the top of the rankings, with the highest success rates within independent schools.
In total, 68 percent of pupils who studied foreign languages achieved a high score.
The data, collected from 74 schools affiliated with SCIS, shows that 72 percent of the students achieved a high score (‘A’) in Mandarin, while 72 percent of students achieved German, 69 percent of students French and 63 percent of Spanish students also achieved an A.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as essential skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages are now valued as much as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in independent school programs and elsewhere.
A survey by the UK Committee on Employment and Skills in 2014 found that of the stumbling blocks to fill vacancies, 17 per cent were attributed to a lack of language skills, according to employers.
Language skills are therefore becoming increasingly important to prepare young people for their future careers.
Now that more and more vacancies require language skills, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the career that someone chooses, if he has learned a second language, in the future he has a real advantage with such a skill for life.
Communicating directly with people from abroad automatically gives a multilingual person an edge over the competition.
According to a YouGov poll among more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 per cent were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation. French was the only tale that stood out above 10 per cent, with a total of 15 percent.
This is why investing in language education is now important for today's children.
Being able to speak multiple languages, particularly that of emerging economies, will give children a better chance of finding a meaningful job.
In Scotland, schools choose which languages they teach.
A number of schools will focus on the more classical modern languages, while other schools will teach languages most important to the UK when they look ahead to 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child’s interest, there will always be different languages to choose from in independent schools, with teachers specializing in this field.
Scottish independent schools are committed to providing a learning environment that prepares children and gives them the skills needed to succeed, regardless of the future.
It cannot currently be denied that languages in a global business environment remain vital to the country's future, so this should be reflected in education.
Modern languages should actually be regarded as ‘international communication skills.’
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
LeBron will make its debut at the Lakers in San Diego on Sunday
Fans awaiting LeBron James' first game at the Los Angeles Lakers, the wait is almost over.
Lakers coach Luke Walton has announced that James will play against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego on Sunday in the opening match before the season.
We still have to determine how many minutes he will play.
“It’ll be more than one, and less than 48,” Walton said on the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers reporter Mike Trudell said in a tweet that James is likely to play for a limited number of minutes.
After training earlier this week, James was asked about his plans for the Lakers' schedule for the six games prior to the season.
'At this stage of my career, I don't need games before the season to prepare,' he said.
Truy of election rallies of Trump in West Virginia, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump is embarking tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia, embarking on a long series of election rallies
It's Trump's first of five scheduled rallies over the next week, including stops in places that are affectionate for him, including Tennessee and Mississippi.
Now that the vote on the appointment of the candidate he prefers for the vacancy in the Supreme Court has been put on hold, Trump wants to build support for the upcoming midterm elections, as Republicans risk losing control of Congress when voted on in November.
What time does Trump's rally in West Virginia start tonight and how do you look online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, starts tonight, Saturday, September 29, 2018 at 7 p.m.
You can watch Trump's rally in West Virginia online via the livestream on YouTube.
Trump is likely to talk about this week's hearings for nominate Chief Justice Brett Kavanaugh. These were extra fraught as Kavanaugh is accused of sexual misconduct and the Senate will wait perhaps a week to vote on his appointment, as the FBI investigates the matter.
But the main objective of this series of rallies is to help the republicans who are candidates for the November election to gain more momentum.
President Trump's campaign leadership said these five rallies in the following week are meant to "encourage volunteers and supporters, while Republicans are trying to protect and expand the majority they have in the Senate and the House of Representatives," Reuters said.
“Congressional control is so important for its program that the president travels to as many states as possible now that the busy campaign season begins,” a spokesman for the Trump campaign who didn’t want to be mentioned by name told Reuters.
Tonight's rally at Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling could attract fans from Ohio and Pennsylvania and be discussed in Pittsburgh's media, West Virginia Metro News said.
Saturday will be the second time in the past month that Trump visited West Virginia, the state he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help the Republican nominee for West Virginia Senate, Patrick Morrisey, who is left behind in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come to give him a boost in the polls,” said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia.
Ryder Cup 2018: Team USA is prepared to fight to keep hope alive in the run-up to Sunday's singles
After three one-sided sessions, Saturday afternoon's foursomes might have been just what this Ryder Cup needed.
The fluctuating sling of opportunities is a completely invented sports concept, but one that players really believe in, and especially in competitions like this.
So who's getting the best chances right now?
“They first had a six-point lead, and now that’s only four more, so we pretty much think luck is on our side right now,” said Jordan Spieth before he left at the end of the day.
Europe, of course, has the advantage that they are four points ahead, while there are still twelve in play.
The Americans, as Spieth said, find that they have a bit of wind in their sails, and there are a lot of things that give courage, not least the shape of Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have been playing together all day and have each scored three points out of four.
Spieth has been an opponent to fear from tee to green and he is setting a good example.
The typical throat sounds he makes when he plays well became louder as his round progressed and he achieved a crucial punt with which match four became a success when he and Thomas were two points behind after two games.
His putt with which they won the match at 15 was followed by a similar scream, the kind from which you can infer that he believes the U.S. team hasn't been beaten yet.
“You have to dig deep and only worry about your own game,” Spieth said.
That's all these players can do now.
18 holes to make your mark.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the last two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the indisputable story of the Ryder Cup.
This curious, but delightful European duo has four out of four and can't do anything wrong.
‘Moliwood’ was the only duo who did not play bogey on Saturday afternoon, but also on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and the back on Friday morning they avoided bogeys.
As a result of that series, and the way they seem to exchange energy with their noisy audience, they are the players who should be defeated on Sunday. If Fleetwood or Molinari wins, he will be the most lest player ever who achieves a European victory when the sun goes down above Le Golf National.
Preferably both at the same time at different holes.
It is premature, however, to already speak of a European victory.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson made shores with Sergio Garcia, that morning's fourball hero, when he formed a duo with Alex Noren.
A bogey and two doubles on the front nine led to a backlog for the Spaniard and the Swede they didn't overcome.
Sunday, however, there's no one who can help to catch up.
The four-balls and foursomes are so fascinating to see through the interactions between the duos, the advice they give, the advice they don’t give and the way a strategy can change in the blink of an eye.
The European team has already played the best so far and has a nice lead in the run-up to the last day, but this foursome session has also shown that Team USA is willing to take the fight, something some, especially in the U.S
Europe takes the lead 10-6 on last day of the Ryder Cup
Europe starts with a nice lead on the last day of the Ryder Cup. After Saturday's four-balls and foursomes, they are 10-6 ahead of the United States.
The animated duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led their team with two wins more than Tiger Woods, who was struggling, giving them up to four-point lead in Le Golf National.
Thomas Bjorn's European team, which the trophy they lost in Hazeltine two years ago, was stronger than the blundering Americans in the four-balls in the morning, with a score of 3-1.
The U.S. offered more resistance in the foursomes, where it won two games, but could no longer catch up with its backlog.
Jim Furyk's team has to score eight points during Sunday's 12 singles matches in order to be able to keep the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European newcomer to win four points in a row, while he and Molinari, who were nicknamed ‘Molliwood’, after a sensational weekend are just the second duo in the history of the Ryder Cup that wins four points in their four opening matches.
Hafting Woods and Patrick Reed into the four-balls, they were beautifully responsive to each other and defeated a disappointing Woods and American newcomer Bryson Dechambeau with a powerful 5&4.
Woods, who dragged himself through two games on Saturday, occasionally showed a dazzling move, but he has now lost 19 of his 29 games in four-balls and foursomes and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, equipped for morning's four-balls, returned to partner Henrik Stenson in the foursomes and together they beat Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, the world's number one and three, with 2&1.
Still, it didn't run from a slate roof for Europe on this pleasant day with a breeze southwest of Paris.
Jordan Spieth, who already won the Major three times, and Justin Thomas set the standard for the Americans on Saturday with three points.
After a difficult match with 2&1 of Spain's Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter in the four-balls and later defeated Poulter and Rory McIlroy with 4&3 in the foursomes, after losing the first four holes.
It's only happened twice before in the history of the Ryder Cup that a team has been able to catch up a backlog of four points at the start of the singles, even though Furyk's team has plenty of draw, as they are currently holding the trophy.
But now that they have been in second place for two days, a counter-attack on Sunday seems too high.
North Korea says it "under no circumstances" will disarm unilaterally without confidence
North Korea's foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that the ongoing sanctions lead to increasing distrust in the United States and that under such circumstances the country could under no circumstances unilaterally give up its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho told the U.N.'s annual General Assembly that North Korea had taken “important goodwill measures” over the past year, such as halting nuclear and missile testing, dismantling the site for nuclear tests, and the promise not to distribute nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“However, we don’t see a corresponding response from the U.S.,” he said.
“Without any trust in the U.S
Although Ri reiterated the well-known North Korean complaints about Washington’s opposition to a “phased” approach to denuclearization, in which North Korea would be rewarded as it takes further steps, it stood out that he disapproved unilateral denuclearization a priori, as Pyongyang used to.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the very first summit between an active U.S. president and a North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim pledged to work towards the “denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,” while Trump pledged assurances for North Korea’s security.
North Korea aims for a formal end of the 1950-53 Korea war, but the United States has said Pyongyangyn must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also opposed callings to ease harsh international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. calls for ‘denuclearization first’ and increases the pressure through sanctions to achieve their goal compellingly and even by objecting to the ‘declaration of the end of war,’” Ri said.
“The perception that sanctions can bring us to our knees is an air castle of the people who are ignorant of us.
But the problem is that the ongoing sanctions only reinforce our distrust.”
Ri made no mention of plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, something the U.S. leader suggested earlier in the week at the United Nations.
Instead, the minister emphasized three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months and added: ‘If the other party on this issue of denuclearization was South Korea and not the U.S., the denuclearisation of the Korean peninsula would not have come to such an impasse.’
Still, the tone of Ri's speech was significantly different from last year, when he said the United Nations General Assembly that it was inevitable that North Korea's missiles on the U.S. mainland would be targeted after 'Mr Evil President' Trump called Kim a 'slit man' on a suicide mission.
Trump, who last year threatened to completely destroy North Korea, praised Kim this year at the United Nations for his courage to take steps to disarm, but said a lot still needs to be done and sanctions must remain until North Korea denuclearizes.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn’t have a timetable for this: “Whether it lasts two years, three years or five months, that doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia believe the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “Enforcing Security Council sanctions must continue vigorously and decisively until we realize the full, final, verified denuclearization.”
Since 2006, the Security Council has unanimously implemented sanctions against North Korea to greatly reduce funding for Pyongyang’s nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo had a meeting with Ri outside the UN General Assembly and said he would go back to Pyongyang next month to make preparations for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last visit did not go well.
Barely several hours after he left Pyongyang in July and said progress had been made, North Korea accused him of making 'gangster-like demands'.
North Korea pledged this month during a meeting with Moon to dismantle a missile site and also a nuclear complex if the United States were to take "corresponding measures."
He said Kim had told him that the “corresponding measures” he wanted were security guarantees Trump had pledged in Singapore, stepping towards normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students take course on the importance of adequate rest
A newing course at Harvard University this year ensures that all students get more sleep, in an effort to combat the growing macho culture in which it seems cool to study all night and drink a lot of caffeine.
A professor determined that the students of the world's largest university often have no idea of the basics of caring for themselves.
Charles Czeisler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at Harvard Medical School a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, drafted the course. As far as he knows, it is unique in the U.S.
He got the idea to start the course after giving a speech about the influence of sleep deprivation on learning.
"At the end of my speech, a girl came to me who said, 'Why don't I just get to hear this right now, in my senior year?'
She said that no one had ever told her how important sleep is, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
In the course, organized for the first time this year, students will explain the basics of how good sleep habits help improve academic and athletic performance and how they also promote their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, said the university decided to organize the course when it was determined that students had a severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The course lasts one hour and includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one of the parts, the students see an image of a dormitory, where they click on coffee cups, curtains, exercise devices and books to learn more about the effects of caffeine and light and how sports performance is affected by sleep deprivation, and the importance of a bedtime routine.
In another component, participants are told how long-term sleep deprivation increases the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
Using a map of the campus, with interactive icons, participants are encouraged to think about their daily routine.
‘We know that we will not immediately change the behaviour of the students.
But we find that they have the right to know all this, just as people have the right to know what consequences the choice for cigarettes has on their health, Czeisler added.
The culture of proudness at a night’s study still exists, he says, and due to modern technology and the increasing pressure on students, sleep deprivation is an ever-increasing problem.
Ensuringing enough sleep, of good quality, must be the student’s ‘secret weapon’ to combat stress, fatigue and anxiety, he said, and even to avoid weight gain, because sleep deprivation causes the brain to give them a constant feeling of hunger.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old from Calfornia who studies chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Czeisler draw up the course. After all, he had taken lessons with the professor last year when he was studying at Harvard in his first year.
He said that the course had opened his eyes and inspired him to ensure that the course became available to everyone on campus.
The next step, he hopes, is to ask all students to pursue a similar study program before coming to the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler advises students to set an alarm for their bedtimes, and not just an alarm to stand on. They should also be aware of the harmful effects of the ‘blue light’ of electronics screens and LED lighting, which can disrupt your circadian rhythm, allowing you to struggle to fall asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's go-along goal makes Gerrard's men go down
The Rangers again saw their away game be disappointing when Dolly Menga scored and defeated Steven Gerrard's weak squad 1-0 in Livingston.
Ibrox's squad hoped for a first win during an away game since they beat St Johnstone 4-1 in February, but Gary Holt's squad ensured the defeat, barely Gerrard's second as manager in 18 games. His squad is now eight points back on Hearts, which runs further at the top of the Ladbrokes Premiership.
Menga scored at seven minutes before halftime, and the Rangers failed to score the equaliser.
The Rangers now drop to sixth, Livingston rises to third, with only a difference in number of goals compared to Hibernian.
And the Rangers could well get into even more trouble, as border judge Calum Spence had to be treated for a head injury after apparently an object was thrown at him by the supporters of the visitor squad.
Gerrard made eight changes to his team. They crossed Ayr and go to the semi-finals of the Betfred Cup.
Holt stayed with the same team for Livingston, who managed to snatch the Hearts a point last week. He would have been delighted about the way his well-trained team managed to unblock the opponents at each move.
The Rangers may have had the most possession, but Livingston did more with it.
They should have scored already after two minutes, when Menga dropped off the ball for the first time and Scott Pittman had Allan McGregor's goal, but the midfielder couldn't finish.
A deep free kick from Keaghan Jacobs reached captain Craig Halkett, but his fellow defender Alan Lithgow kicked against the back post.
The Rangers did manage to take control, but they seemed to be hoping for a good outcome rather than they really believed in it if they could approach the target.
Alfredo Morelos, in any case, felt he was entitled to a penalty kick when he and Steven Lawless collided after a quarter of an hour, but referee Steven Thomson dismissed the Colombian's request.
The Rangers could only shoot to the goal twice in the first half, but Liam Kelly, who used to be goalkeeper with the Ibrox, was able to stop Lassana Coulibaly's header and Ovie Ejaria's tame shot with ease.
Livingston's opener in the 34th minute may not be in line with the overall course of the game, no one can deny that they deserved this, if only for their hard work.
Once again the Rangers weren't able to cope with Jacobs' deep game pattern.
Scott Arfield didn't respond when Declan Gallagher passed the ball to Scott Robinson, who remained cold-blooded and chose Menga, who kicked the ball into goal with ease.
Gerrard ensured an interruption with the substitute Coulibaly/Ryan Kent. This move had almost immediate consequences when the winger Morelos passed, but an impressive Kelly raced away from his line to stop the ball.
But Livingston kept forcing visitors to play the kind of match Livingston prefers to play, with Lithgow and Halkett continuing to kick long balls.
Holt's team could have heightened his lead in the final stages of the match, but McGregor held up well and blocked Jacobs, after which Lithgow came wide from the corner.
Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton of the Rangers asked for a penalty kick at the end of the match after a collision with Jacobs, but Thomson looked the other way again.
Almanac: the inventor of the Geiger counter
And now a page from the almanac of our ‘Sunday Morning’: September 30, 1882, today exactly 136 years ago, for those who can COULD COUNT... the day when the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm ‘Hans’ Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the device named geiger counter.
The geiger counter has since been indispensable from science, and also became an icon in pop culture, for example in ‘Bells or Coronado’, a film from 1950 with the at first sight unlikely cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: ‘What the hell is that?’
Rogers: ‘That’s a geiger counter that is used to detect radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
When you set up these headphones, you hear the effect of the atoms that are released by the radioactivity into the minerals.”
Evans: ‘It’s really banging now!’
‘Hans’ Geiger died in 1945, barely a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name remains relevant.
New cancer vaccine may teach the immune system to “see” malignant cells
New cancer vaccine could teach the immune system to “see” and kill malignant cells
Vaccine Teaches Immuness to Recognize malignant Cells in the context of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells in a patient to modify them in a lab
They can then ‘see’ a protein that is common in many types of cancer and are then re-injected
A test vaccine yields promising results in patients with different types of cancer.
A woman who was treated with the vaccine that teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells saw her ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months
With the method, immune cells are removed in a patient, after which the cells in the lab are adjusted in such a way that they can ‘see’ the protein HER2, which often occurs with all kinds of cancers. Then the cells are injected again.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: “Our results show that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 ‘stimulates the growth of different types of cancer’, including breast cancer, ovarian cancer, lung cancer and colon cancer, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar way to remove immune cells from patients and “teach” these cells to tackle cancer cells has previously been found to work in the treatment of a certain type of leukaemia.
Kanye West stabs pro-Trump tirade and wears MAGA cap after his appearance in Saturday Night Live.
That didn't work out the way he hoped
Kanye West was booed during a broadcast of Saturday Night Live following an incoherent performance in which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump and said he will become a presidential candidate in 2020.
After bringing his third song of the evening, Ghost Town, bearing a cap on his head with the lyrics Make America Great Again, he stabbed a tirade against the Democrats and reiterated that he supports Trump.
‘Often when I’m talking to a white person, he asks, ‘How can you have sympathy for Trump now? He’s a racist!’
“If I was worried about racism, I’d have left America for a long time,” he said.
The broadcast of Saturday Night Live began with a parody in which Hollywood star Matt Damon stabbed the dragon with Brett Kavanaugh's testimony in the Senate Justice Committee in connection with the alleged sexual assault of which Christine Blasey Ford accuses him.
West's tirade didn't air, but comedian Chris Rock uploaded them on social media.
It's not clear if Rock with his post wants to make fun of West.
West also complained to the public that he had been taken backstage under fire for his cap.
“They harassed me backstage.
They said, 'Don't get on stage with that cap.'
They've harassed me!
And then they say I'm apathetic," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued: ‘Do you want to see how apathetic looks?’ He said he was going to attract his superman-cape, ‘because that means you can’t command me. Do you want the world to get better?
Give some love.”
From the audience at least twice booing and the cast members of SNL seemed embarrassed, according to Variety. The magazine was told by one present: ‘The whole studio was suddenly silent.’
West served as a last-minute replacement for singer Ariana Grande, whose ex-boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West surprised many with a performance of the song I Love it while dressed as a Perrier bottle.
West received support from the leader of the conservative group TPUSA, Candace Turner, who tweeted: ‘To one of our bravest minds: THANK YOU THE MASSA PROUD.’
But talkshow presenter Karen Hunter tweeted that West was “just herself, and that’s beautiful.”
“But I choose not to reward someone who harms, supports and spits out an ideology that harms my community (by buying his music or clothes or supporting his ‘art’).
He's free to do whatever he wants.
Us too," she added.
Before the show began, the rapper had announced on Twitter that he had changed his name. From now on, he says, is ‘the orphans formally known as Kanye West’.
He's not the first artist to change his name. He was preceded by Diddy Puff, also known as Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Colleague rapper Snoop Dogg has also worn the name Snoop Lion and the late music legend Prince once changed his name into a symbol and then into The artist formerly known as Prince.
Accusation of attempted murder after stabbing at Belfast restaurant
A 45-year-old man was charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed Friday at a restaurant in eastern Belfast.
The incident took place in Ballyhackamore, police said.
The defendant is expected to appear before the police judge in Belfast on Monday.
The allegation will be investigated by the Public Prosecution Service.
Game of Thrones star Kit Harington lashes out at toxic masculiness
Kit Harington is known for his role of swordsman Jon Snow in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series Game of Thrones.
But the 31-year-old actor lashes out at the stereotype of the macho hero and says young boys when seeing such roles on the screen often think they have to be tough to gain respect.
In a conversation with The Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he feels “something has gone wrong” and questioned how the problem of toxic masculiness should be tackled in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who has recently been married to his co-star of Game of Thrones, Rose Leslie, who is also 31, admitted that he is strongly convinced that the problem needs to be addressed.
'This is really an important question for me personally at the moment: where did it go wrong with our manhood?' he said.
'What did we teach men when they grew up, which is why we're facing this problem now?'
Kit thinks that television might be partly responsible for the rise in toxic masculinity, due to his very male characters.
He continued: “What is innate and what has been learned?”
What is taught on TV and on the streat that makes young boys think they have to be a certain kind of man?
I think that's one of the big questions of today: how do we change that?
“There’s clearly something going wrong for young men.”
In the interview, he also admitted that he won't act in prequels or sequels to Games of Thrones when the series ends next summer, because he's "get ready with battlefields and horses."
Beginning in November, Kit stars in a revival of True West by Sam Shepard, the story of a film producer and his brother, who is robber.
The actor recently revealed that he feels that his encounter with his wife Rose is the most beautiful result of Game of Thrones.
'I met my wife during the series, so that's how I've left my future family and my living from now on,' he said.
Rose played Ygritte, the great love of Kit's character Jon Snow, in the fantasy series that was crowned an Emmy.
The couple got married in June 2018 at the Leslies family estate in Scotland.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% new cases increase
China reports that the number of Chinese with HIV and AIDS has increased by 14%.
Health authorities say more than 820,000 people have been affected by it in the country.
40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
In the vast majority of new cases, the diseases were transmitted through sex, which was not the case before.
HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China by transfusions of contaminated blood.
But the number of people contracting HIV in this way is now almost zero, Chinese health officials said during a conference in Yunnan province.
Compared to the same period last year, however, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increased by 100,000 people.
The transmission of HIV through sex is an acute problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality is no longer punishable in China since 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is reportedly widespread.
Due to country's conservative values, according to studies, an estimated 70 to 90% of men who have sex with men would eventually marry a woman.
Often the diseases are transmitted by inadequate protection in sexual relations in these relationships.
The Chinese government has been promising universal access to HIV medication since 2003 in the context of efforts to address the issue.
Maxine Waters denies that staffer leaked data from Republican senators and lashes out at 'dangerous lies' and 'conspiracy theories'
U.S. congressman Maxine Waters goes against the accusations that one of her staffers has posted the personal records of three U.S. Republican senators on the lawmakers' Wikipedia page.
The Los Angeles Democrat claims the allegations are spread by "far-right-righted" know-it-alls and websites.
"Lies, lies and even more mean lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
Among other things, the published data would include the private address and private phone number of U.S. Senator Lindsey Graham from South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The data surfaced online Thursday. They were posted by an unidentifier in Capitol Hill during a Senate-committee's hearing on the allegations of sexual misconduct to Brett Kavanaugh
The leak came after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
According to conservative websites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState, the IP address indicating the source of the posts was linked to Waters's office and contained the information of an employee of Waters, the Hill said.
“This unfounded accusation is completely false and a blatant lie,” Waters said.
‘My employee, whose identity, personal data and security have been compromised as a result of these false accusations, was in no way responsible for leaking this information.
This unfounded allegation is completely false and a blatant lie.”
Waters's statement soon came across criticism online, including by former White House press officer Ari Fleischer.
"This denial sounds angry," Fleischer wrote.
“You can conclude that she doesn’t have the temperament to be a congressman.
If someone is accused of something he hasn't done, he shouldn't get angry.
He shouldn't get rebellious.
He shouldn't be questioning the prosecutor's motives.
He has to remain calm and serene.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters's response to Democrats' criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused by critics of exaggerated anger during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republican nominee who wants to take Water’s place in the midterm elections, also gave his opinion on Twitter.
“Violent, if it’s true,” he tweeted.
In her statement, Waters said her office had notified the relevant services and law enforcement agencies of these false allegations.
“We’ll ensure that the perpetrators become known,” she said, “and that they are legally held accountable for all their actions that are devastating and dangerous for my staffers.”
Review: Johnny English Strikes Again - Weak Spy Parody with Rowan Atkinson
In every new film with a British influence, we are now traditionally looking for references to Brexit, and that turns out to apply to this revival of the Johnny English franchise, the comedy action films with a weak spy in the lead, which started in 2003 with Johnny English and was recalled with Johnny English Reborn in 2011.
Is this airy self-statire about how bad we're doing the latest export product for our country?
The incompetent Johnny English with bulging eyes and flexible facial features at least manages to steer everything in the hundred, also with his second comeback. From his name you can already infer that he is a generally comical character that was conceived for moviegoers in countries where one speaks a language other than English.
He's obviously the foolish secret cop who, despite his bizarre claim to slick glamour, a little Clouseau, a touch of Mr Bean and an ounce of that bloke who contributed one note to Chariots of Fire's theme song during the opening ceremony of the London Olympics in 2012.
He's originally based on the traveler and the international mysterious man Atkinson once played in Barclaycard's TV spots (which everyone has meanwhile been forgotten) that always leaves a certain chaos.
His latest portrait of Johnny English contains one or two beautiful moments.
I thought it was wonderful how Johnny English approaches a helicopter in his medieval armor and taps the rotor blades against his helmet.
Atkinson can clearly show in this film how much talent he has for physical comedy, but the humour comes across rather weak and crazy enough excessive, especially now that the “serious” film series like 007 and Mission Impossible themselves include comedy as an ingredient, and doing that right.
The humor seems more intended for children than for adults, and Johnny English's mad calamity comes across to me less original and focused than the jokes without text from the character Bean playing Atkinson.
The constant theme now is that Britain is in great difficulty.
A cyberhacker has infiltrated the super-secret spy network on the web, revealing the identity of all of Britain's active secrecy agents, much to the agent's disqualification in office, a sadly small role of Kevin Eldon.
That’s the drop that makes the bucket overflow from the prime minister, a pretentious woman with problems on her head and terribly unpopular as a politician. Emma Thompson does her utmost with this character reminiscent of Teresa May, but the script doesn’t provide much material to work with.
According to her intelligence advisers, she has to deploy a retired spy, since every active spy is compromised.
And that means the bunctulating Johnny English himself, who now works as a schoolmaster in a fancy institution, but unofficially teaches about undercover work. There are nice jokes in the story here, because English's espionage lessons remind something of School of Rock.
English is quickly summoned at Whitehall for an emergency briefing and reunited with his former pathetic sidekick Bough, replayed by Ben Miller.
Bough is now a married man. His wife is captain of a submarine, a boisterous and jovial role that lies below Vicki Pepperdine's level.
The Batman and Robin of the disastrous states of Her Majesty's Secret Service are thus back in action and meet Olga Kurylenko's beautiful femme fatale Ophelia Bulletova.
Meanwhile, the prime minister unfortunately falls for the charms of the charismatic technology billionaire who claims he can solve the British computer problems: the sinister Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough embark on their odyssey of farcical fun: dressed up as waiters they set fire to a flashy French restaurant, they shovel noise aboard Volta’s luxury hunt and English unleashes pure anarchy when he tries to use a Virtual Reality headset to familiarize themselves with the interior of Volta’s house.
For the latter, all registers are opened, but no matter how well-intentioned and rambunctious the scene is, she is reminiscent of children's television.
It's all not very original.
And as with Johnny English's other films, I'm asking myself the question: can't the British film secret really come up with a role that honors Rowan Atkinson's talent?
Labour denies that the party is devising a plan in which Britons work four days a week but get paid for five days
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party would consider a radical plan in which Britons work four days a week but getting paid for five days.
Reportedly, the party would like bosses of companies to pass on the savings they make as a result of the revolution of Artificial Intelligence (A.I.) to their employees by giving them an extra day of vacation.
For example, employees can enjoy a three-day weekend, but still earn the same amount.
According to sources, the idea “fits” into the party’s economic agenda and plans to make the country’s balance tilt towards the workers.
The transition to a four-day working week has already been approved by the Trade Union Confederation, which considers it a good way to ensure that workers can take advantage of the changing economy.
A high-ranking source from the Labour party told The Sunday Times: 'A policy review is expected to be announced before the end of the year.
"It won't happen overnight, but a four-day working week is a goal that fits within the party's aspiration to make the balance in the economy strike more for the benefit of the workers and that also fits within the party's overall industrial strategy."
Labour is not the first party to support such an idea, given that the Green Party already promised a four-day working week in the 2017 national election campaign.
However, the aspiration is currently not yet supported by the entire Labour Party.
A Labour Party spokesman said: "A four-day working week is not a policy intention of the party and is also not considered by the party."
Shadow Chancellor John McDonnell used Labor's conference last week to clarify his vision of a socialist revolution in the economy.
McDonnell said he was determined to take back power from the 'faceless directors' and the 'profiters' at utilities.
The Shadow Chancellor's plans also mean that the current shareholders of water companies may not get back the full value of their share, because a Labour government could apply 'discounts' based on perceived misconduct.
He also confirmed that there are plans to include employees on the boards of companies and to set up Inclusive Ownership Funds (Inclusive Ownership Funds) with which 10 percent of the shares of private services are given to the employees who can earn up to £500 a year.
Lindsey Graham and Johnenge tell '60 Minutes' whether the FBI's investigation into Kavanaugh could change their minds
The FBI's investigation into indictments against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has delayed a final vote on his nomination before the Supreme Court by at least one week and raises the question of whether the FBI's findings could cause Republican senators to withdraw their support for the candidate.
In an interview aired at '60 Minutes' on Sunday, correspondent Scott Pelley asked Republican Senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham whether the FBI could discover anything that could lead them to change their minds.
Kennedy seemed more open in this than his colleague from South Carolina.
"I mean, of course," Kennedy said.
"I said before the hearing that I have spoken to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him, after this happened, after the accusation came out, and I asked him 'Did you do it?'
“He was resolute, determined, unambiguous.”
Graham's voice, however, seems to be stuck.
"My opinion of Brett Kavanaugh is certain, and the allegation should contain dynamite," he said.
"As far as Dr. Ford is concerned, I don't know what happened, but I know one thing: Brett has vigorously denied it," Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
"And no one who has been named by her could verify it.
- It's been 36 years.
“I don’t see new developments.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival and has it contributed to reducing poverty?
This Saturday the Global Citizen Festival will be organized in New York, an annual music event where a very impressive line-up of stars will perform and which has an equally impressive mission: the end of poverty in the world.
For the seventh time again, tens of thousands of people for the Global Citizen Festival will come to the Great Lawn in Central Park. Not only to enjoy acts such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes but also to generate awareness for the real goal: ending extreme poverty for 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, first organized in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, a group of international advocacies who hope to end poverty by increasing the number of people actively battling against it.
Concertgoers who want to obtain a free ticket for the event (unless they were willing to pay for a VIP ticket), had to complete a series of tasks, or ‘actions’, such as volunteering, e-mailing a world leader, making a phone call, or otherwise meaningfully generating awareness for purpose: an end to poverty.
But how successful has the Global Citizen Festival been, with 12 years to go to achieve this goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert a suitable way to convince people to demand a call to action or is this just another form of ‘clicktivism’, giving people the feeling that they are making a real contribution by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
More than 19 million actions by supporters have been registered since 2011, according to Global Citizen, aimed at a large number of different targets.
According to Global Citizen, these actions have contributed to the fact that world leaders have committed and made policy proposals worth more than 37 billion dollars that must change the lives of 2.25 billion people by 2030.
Early in 2018, the group cited 390 initiatives and statements based on the actions, of which at least 10 billion dollars have already been paid out or brought together by sponsor actions.
The group estimates that the funds obtained so far have had an impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Among the most important declarations of intent are those of The Power of Nutrition, a partnership of investors and executives from the United Kingdom with the aim of ‘helping children realize their full potential’. This group has pledged 35 million dollars to Rwanda to help end malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
"With the help of the British government, from donors, from other national governments and from Global Citizens like you, we can turn the social injustice that is malnutrition into history," Tracey Ullman, ambassador for The Power of Nutrition, told the public at a concert in London in April 2018.
The group also announced that, after more than 5,000 actions had been sented in which the British government was asked to improve the nutritional situation for mothers and children, the government has announced to support a project, the Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children with dietary interventions.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on the Global Citizen's website asking "why do you think we can put an end to extreme poverty?"
The group replied: "It's going to be a long and difficult road, where sometimes we'll fall and fail.
But, just like civil rights and the anti-apartheid movements ahead of us, we'll ultimately be successful because we're stronger together.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are a few of the performers who will perform this year at the New York event presented by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The United States could deploy the navy for a 'blockade' to impede Russian energy exports - interior minister
Washington may 'if necessary' resort to the navy to prevent Russian energy from entering the market, including in the Middle East, stated Ryan Zinke, the U.S. Secretary of the Interior, as stated by the Washington Examiner.
Zinke claimed that Russia's involvement in Syria, where the country acts at the invitation of the legal government, is a pretext for exploring new energy markets.
"I believe they are only in the Middle East in order to be able to act in energy, just like in Eastern Europe and the southern flank of Europe," he said.
And according to the official, there are ways and means to counter this.
"The United States has that capability, with the navy, to ensure that the sailing routes remain open and, if necessary, to block them to ensure that their energy does not enter the market," he said.
Zinke spoke to invitees at an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-for-profit group that advertises itself as the 'voice of the energy consumer' in the U.S.
He went further and compared Washington's approach of Russia to Iran's approach and noted that these do not differ materially.
"The economic option for Iran and Russia is more or less the use and replacement of fuels," he said and referred to Russia as a country that only has an "art", with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
These statements come at a time when the Trump administration is attempting to boost exports of liquefied natural gas to Europe, replacing Russia, a country that offers by far the cheapest option for European consumers.
For that reason, officials of the Trump administration, including President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to withdraw sight from the 'unsuitable' pipeline project Nord Stream 2, which according to Trum of Berlin has made the 'prisoner' of Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly emphasized that the Nord Stream 2-pipeline, worth 11 billion dollars through which the current pipeline capacity will be doubled to 110 billion cubic meters, is purely an economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's powerful opposition to the project will only be driven by economic reasons and that it is an example of unfair competition.
"I believe we agree with the view that energy should not be used to exert pressure and that consumers should be able to choose their suppliers themselves" said Aleksandr Novak, Russia's energy minister, after a meeting with Rick Perry, the U.S
The U.S
The Bundesverband der Deutschen Industrie (BDI), Germany's leading industrial organization, has urged the U.S. not to interfere with the EU's energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"I have a big problem when a third power interferes with our energy supply," Dieter Kempf, the head of the Bundesverband der Deutschen Industrie (BDI) said after a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren 'thinks about it seriously' to take part in the presidential election in 2020, the senator for Massachusetts says
Elizabeth Warren, the senator from Massachusetts said on Saturday that she "seriously thinks about" taking part in the presidential elections after the midterm elections.
During a local meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed she's thinking about taking part in the election.
"It's about time that women go to Washington to repair the damaged government, and this also entails that a woman is in the top position," she said, according to the political news magazine The Hill.
"After November 6, I'll start seriously thinking about running for president."
Warren sailed against President Donald Trump at the meeting in the town hall and said he is "steering this country in the wrong direction."
"I'm troubled to the blunt because of what Donald Trump brings about in our democracy," she said.
Warren has been candid in her criticism of Trump and Brett Kavanaugh, his candidate for the Supreme Court.
In a tweet on Friday, Warren said "of course we have to await an investigation from the FBI before we vote on this."
However, an opinion survey published on Thursday found that a majority of Warren's own supporters feel she shouldn't stand for election by 2020.
Fifty-eight percent of Massachusetts 'probable' voters said the senator shouldn't stand for election, according to an opinion survey by the Political Research Center at Suffolk University and the Boston Globe.
Thirty-two percent said she'll be eligible.
The investigation showed that there was more support for former Governor Deval Patrick, with 38 percent ahead of a possible candidacy and 48 percent against.
Other names of leading Democrats associated with a possible participation in the 2020 presidential election include former Vice President Joe Biden and Bernie Sanders, senator from Vermont.
Biden said he would officially make a decision in January, according to an Associated Press report.
Sarah Palin speaks over PTSD from Track Palin at Donald Trump's election rally
Track Palin, aged 26, has spent a year in Iraq after he enlisted in September.
He has been arrested and indicted after a report of domestic violence Monday night.
"Because of what my own son has to experience, what he's going through now that he's back, I understand other families who suffer the consequences of PTSD and the injuries with which some of our soldiers return" she told the audience that was present at a meeting for Donald Trump in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called his arrest "the elephant in the room" and said of her son and other veterans "they come back a little differently, they come back and have become tougher, they come back and wonder if there is respect for what their fellow soldiers and the pilots and all the other soldiers have given for the homeland".
He was arrested Monday in Wasilla, Alaska and has been charged with domestic violence and assault on a woman, interference in a report on domestic violence and possession of a weapon under the influence, according to Dan Bennett, police spokesman in Wasilla.
Washington D.C. and 18 states are supporting the protest against a new asylum policy
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia support a legal protest against a new policy in the U.S
Representatives of the 18 states and Washington D.C. filed a statement last Friday as 'friend-of-the-court' filed a statement with the Washington court in support of an asylum seeker contesting this policy, NBC News reports.
The complainant's full name in the Grace versus Sessions case the American Civil Liberties Union filed against federal policy in August has not been disclosed.
She says her partner "and his violent sons who are members of a gang" abused her but that U.S. officials rejected her asylum application on July 20.
She's detained in Texas.
The lawyers of the states that support Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, from which come a large number of people who apply for asylum in the U.S., as countries that have major problems related to gangs and domestic violence.
U.S.'s new asylum policy has rolled back a decision by the Board of Immigrant Appeals from 2014, making it possible for immigrants without documents fleeing domestic violence to apply for asylum.
Karl Racine, the attorney general of Washington D.C., said Friday in a statement that the new policy "ignores decades of state, federal and international law."
"Federal legislation requires that all asylum applications be assessed on the basis of the specific facts and circumstances of the application and such an impediment is contrary to that principle" is stated in declaration by the 'friend-of-the-court.'
The lawyers continue to argue that the policy denying access to the U.S
Jeff Sessions, the justice secretary, in June ordered judges no longer granted asylum to victims of domestic and gang-related violence.
"Asylum is available to those fleeing their country because of persecution or fear based on race, religion, nationality or membership of a specific social group or political view," Sessions said in its June 11 announcement of policies.
Asylum was never meant to alleviate all problems, even if these problems are serious, what people face every day, all over the world.
Desperate rescue efforts in Palu while the number of deaths doubled in the race to find survivors
For the survivors, the situation also got worse.
"It feels very tense" the 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma said, as she tried to comfort her feverous baby at an evacuation center in the devastated town of Palu.
“Every minute, new corpses arrive in ambulances.
And clean water is scarce."
Residents came back to their destroyed homes and searched in their thornetated possessions for valuables that they could still use.
Hundreds of people have suffered injuries and the hospitals are overcrowded, insofar as they have not been damaged by the 7.5 earthquake on Richter's scale.
Some injured, including Dwi Haris who sustained a fractured back and shoulder, rested outside the army hospital in Palu, where patients were treated outdoors due to the likelihood of heavy aftershocks.
Tears filled his eyes as he recounted his feelings when the devastating earthquake shook the hotel room on the fifth floor, which he shared with his wife and daughter.
“There wasn’t time to save ourselves.
I was pressed against the remnants of a wall," Haris told a correspondent of the Associated Press and added to that he was with his family in town for a wedding.
"I heard my wife calling for help, and later I didn't hear anything.
- I don't know what's happened to her and our child.
“I hope they’re safe.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of 'intimidation' with 'propaganda ads'
A week after an official Chinese news magazine placed a four-page advertisement in a U.S. daily newspaper touting the mutual benefits of U.S.-China commerce, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump recently referred to China Daily's paid appendix in the Des Moines Register, the best-selling newspaper in Iowa, having accused China of interfering in the November 6 U.S. congressional elections, an allegation denied by China.
Trump's allegation that Beijing was trying to interfere with the U.S
Although it's normal for foreign governments to place ads to promote commerce, Beijing and Washington are currently in an escalating trade war, in which they've imposed ever-increasing import tariffs on each other.
China's retaliatory tariffs early in the trade war were specifically aimed at striking exporters in states such as Iowa, who support Trump's Republican Party, as Chinese and U.S. experts have said.
Terry Branstad, the U.S.'s ambassador to China and previously long-standing governor of Iowa, a key exporter of agricultural products to China, said China had affected U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
"China," as Branstad wrote in Sunday's opinion piece in Des Moine's Register, "now doubles the intimidation by publishing propaganda ads in our free press."
“By spreading this propaganda, the government of China uses freedom of expression and a free press, a long cherished tradition in America by placing a paid advertisement in Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
"In contrast, at a kiosk in central Beijing you will find in vain deviant voices and you will not see a true reflection of the divisive opinions that the Chinese population may have about the difficult economic trajectory, because the media are firmly under the thumb of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's most prominent news magazines has refused to publish his article," as he did not mention which news magazine this was.
Through the Kavanaugh debacle, Republicans alienate female voters just before the midterm elections, analysts warn
Now that many important Republicans are rallying around Brett Kavanaugh, the candidate for the Supreme Court, and defending him against various allegations of sexual violence, analysts warn that they will experience a setback, particularly among women, during the upcoming midterm elections.
The emotions around this topic have risen enormously and most Republicans have already hinted that they want to continue with the mood.
"These things can't be rolled back," Grant Reeher, professor of politicology at Syracuse University's Maxwell School, told The Hill for an article published on Saturday.
Reeher said he wonders whether Senator Jeff Flake's attempt to plead for an FBI investigation at the last minute will be enough to satisfy angry voters.
"Women are not going to forget what happened yesterday, they have not forgotten it tomorrow and they have not forgotten it in November," Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and national spokeswoman for the progressive group MoveOn on Friday, according to the news magazine from Washington, D.C.
On Friday morning, protesters shouted "November is coming!" during their demonstration in the hall of the Senate while the Republicans who control the Justice Committee chose to approve Kavanaugh's candidacy, despite the testimony of Dr Christine Blasey Ford, Mic reported.
"The enthusiasm and motivation for the Democrats will reach a record high," Stu Rothenberg, an impartial political analyst, told the news site.
"People say it's already high, and that's true.
But it can add up even further, particularly among floating female voters in the suburbs and among younger voters, aged 18 to 29, who often don't vote even though they disagree with the president."
Even for Ford's public testimony, in which she further explained her accusation of sexual harassment against the Supreme Court candidate, analysts suggested there could be a recoil if Republicans pushed through the nomination.
"This has become a confused mess for the Republican Party," said Michael Steele, former party chairman of the Republican Party, earlier last week according to NBC News.
"It's not just about the vote in the committee, or about the final vote or Kavanaugh is taking place in court, but it's also about the way the Republicans have handled this and how they've handled her" Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA, a group that helps Democrats get elected, makes clear to the news channel.
However, Americans turn out to be divided over the question of who they believe after the testimony of Ford and Kavanaugh, in which a small majority signs up for the latter.
A new YouGov opinion survey shows that 41 percent of respondents certainly or probably believe Ford's testimony and that 35 percent definitely or probably believe Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38 percent said they thought Kavanaugh certainly or probably lied during his testimony while only 30 percent said the same thing about Ford.
Following Flake's pass, the FBI is currently investigating Ford's accusations and at least one other prosecutor, Deborah Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Ford last week before the Senate Judiciary Committee under oath stated that Kavanaugh sexually assaulted her under the influence of liquor when she was 17 years old.
Ramirez alleges that the candidate for the Supreme Court has showned his genitals to her during a party at the time they both studied at Yale in the 80s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web is planning a new internet to tackle Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, begins a startup that aims to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt, the technology legend's latest project, is a company that is based on Berners-Lee's open source platform Solid.
Solid offers users the possibility to choose where their data is stored and which persons have access to which information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that 'world domination' is the guiding idea behind Inrupt.
"We have to do this now," he said about the startup.
"This is a historic moment."
The app leverages Solid's technology to enable people to create their own 'personal online data storage' or POD.
It stores lists of contacts, activity lists, a calendar, a music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available together through one browser and all at the same time.
What makes POD unique is the fact that it depends entirely on the user who has access to what information.
The company calls this "personal empowerment through data."
The idea behind Inrupt, according to CEO John Bruce, is that the company delivers the resources, processes and necessary skills to make Solid available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, some developers contracted to work on the project, and a community of volunteer programmers.
As of this week, developers of technology around the world can create their own decentralized apps with the tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team aren't going to have conversations with "Facebook and Google about whether or not to introduce a complete transformation through which all their business models are turned completely upside down at once."
"We're not asking for their permission."
In a post on Medium published Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that the "mission (of Inrupt) is to deliver the commercial energy and an ecosystem that helps protect the integrity and quality of the new web, which is being built on Solid."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate on net neutrality.
Also after the start of Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain as founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this new era for the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: cleric and bearer of the Victoria Cross from World War I honored
The only cleric of the Church of England who acquired a Victoria Cross during the First World War as a military is honored 100 years later in his birthplace.
Lieutenant-colonel, the Reverend Bernard Vann obtained the award in response to the attack at Bellenglise and Lehaucourt on 29 September 1918.
However, he was killed four days later by a sniper and thus never knew that he had the highest British military honour.
A commemorative stone was unveiled by its two grandsons during a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire, on Saturday.
One of the grandsons, Michael Vann, said it was 'brilliantly symbolic' that the stone was unveiled exactly 100 years after the fact for which his grandfather had been distinguished.
According to the London Gazette, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion on 29 September 1918 over the Canal de Saint-Quentin "in a very dense fog and under fierce fire of field guns and machine guns."
He later stormed through to the line of fire and led the line forward before running alone to a field gun and eliminating three members of the detachiement.
Lieutenant-Colonel Vann was killed by a German sharpshooter on October 4, 1918, just over one month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather's actions were "something that I know I'll never match, but also something that makes me feel humbly."
He and his brother Dr. James Vann also laid another wreath after the parade led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said he "felt very honored to be allowed to play a role in the parade" adding that "the prowess of a true hero is evidenced by the support he receives from a large number of people."
Fans of MMA stayed up all night to see Bellator 206 and got Peppa Pig instead
Imagine, you've stayed awake all night to watch a stuffed Bellator 206 and you won't get to see the most important moment.
On the San Jose billboard there were 13 fights listed, including six in the main section, and this was broadcast all night in the UK on Channel 5.
At 6:00 in the morning, just when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were getting ready for their fight, viewers in the United Kingdom were uncomfortably surprised when the transmitter switched to Peppa Pig.
Some were indignant because they had stayed awake all night especially for this fight.
One fan described the transition to the children's program on Twitter as "a bad joke."
"According to government regulations, after 6:00 a.m in the morning, no content that is unsuitable for children, and therefore we had to switch to the children's program," said Dave Schwartz, senior vice president for marketing and communications at Bellator, when asked about the broadcast.
""Peppa the pig," yesss."
Scott Coker, Bellator's corporate chairman, said they're going to adjust their programming to ensure that viewers in the UK will also get a chance in the future.
"I think, in view of the return match, that we can solve this problem" said Coker.
"But it's six o'clock in the morning here on a Sunday and we can't discuss anything until Sunday our time, Monday their time.
- We're working on it.
When I made the switch, a lot of texts went back and forth and believe me, these weren't all equally friendly.
We were trying to solve it, we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn't, it was a government problem.
- I can promise this won't happen a second time.
We're limiting the broadcasts to five nights instead of six, as we normally already did, we tried to make it even better for the fans, but we just went too far.
It's an unfortunate situation."
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt 'inferior' because of his sexual orientation
Olympic jumper Tom Daley says he felt inferior during his childhood because of his sexual orientation, but that this gave him the motivation to become successful.
The twenty-four-year-old said, until he went to secondary education, he didn't realize that "not everyone is like me."
In a conversation with Desert Island Discs on Radio 4 with Lauren Laverne, he said he had spoken out about gay rights to give others 'hope'.
He also said that parenting had contributed to placing less value on winning during the Olympics.
Kirsty Young, the regular presenter of the long-running show has taken a few months off because of illness.
Appearing as an outcast on Laverne's first programme, Daley said he felt 'less' than others during his childhood because it was 'socially unaccepted to like both girls and boys'.
He said, "Until today, those feelings, the feeling of being less and the feeling of being different, are really existing facts that have given me the power to be successful."
He wanted to prove that he was "someone" so he wouldn't disappoint people if they ended up finding out his orientation.
The winner of two bronze Olympic medals has become a notable campaigner for the LGBT community and used his presence at the Commonwealth Games in Australia this year to call on more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he spoke out because he was happy that he could live openly without any further consequences and that he wanted to give others 'hope'.
The three-time world champion said the fact that he fell in love with a man, American filmmaker Dustin Lance Back, whom he met in 2013, has 'completely surprised me'.
Daley was married to the Oscar winner last year, who is 20 years older than him, but said the age difference had never been a problem.
"When you've endured so much so young," he competed in his first Olympics at the age of fourteen, and his father died of cancer three years later, it's hard to find someone in your own age group who's experienced the same highs and lows.
The pair have become parents in June of a son named Robert Ray Black-Daley and Daley said his "whole perspective" had changed.
"If you'd asked me this question last year, I'd have replied that it was all about winning a gold medal" he said.
"But, you know what, there's more important business on earth than winning gold Olympic medals.
“My gold Olympic medal is Robbie.”
His son has the same name as his father who died in 2011 at the age of 40, having been diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father hadn't accepted that he'd die and one of the last things he'd asked was if they'd already received the tickets for London 2012, because he wanted to sit in the front row.
"I couldn't get it over my heart to say, you won't be there anymore to sit in the front rhye, Dad," he said.
"I held his hand when he stopped breathing and not until his breathing actually stopped and he passed away, I could finally acknowledge that he wasn't invincible" he said.
The following year, Daley competed in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
"I knew I'd been dreaming about this all my life. Diving in front of a home audience during the Olympics, there's no better feeling" he said.
It was also the inspiration for his first choice of music, Proud by Heather Small, that had gripped him strongly on his way to the games and where he still got goosebumps.
Desert Island Discs can be listened to on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 BST.
Mickelson, not in shape, on bench during the Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mickelson will set a record on Sunday when he plays his 47th game in the Ryder Cup, but he has to find his form back so as not to make a sad milestone out of it.
Mickelson, who is competing in the biennial event for the 12th time, a record, was placed on the bench bench for the fourballs and foursomes on Saturday.
Instead of being at the center of action, as he's often done for the United States, the winner of five majors divides the day between supporting his teammates and practicing on the driving range, in an attempt to figure out what's going on.
'Cause, even at the peak of his career, he was never known for his straight drives, the 48-year-old is not the ideal choice for the close fairways of Le Golf National, where the long grass of the roughs wander balls routinely punishes.
And if the track itself isn't deterrent enough, Mickelson in the ninth match on Sunday the ever-accurate Francesco Molinari, winner of the British Open, against who's coupled with newcomer Tommy Fleetwood to win all four matches this week.
If the Americans who are four points behind at the start of the 12 single matches, Mickelson's match can be absolutely crucial.
Furyk has proved to have confidence in this man, although frankly he had a hard time doing otherwise.
"He completely understood the role he had today, gave me a slap on the back, put his arm around me and said he'll be ready tomorrow," Furyk said.
"He has a lot of faith in himself.
It's inducted into the Hall of Fame and he's contributed so much to these teams, in the past, but also this week.
I probably didn't think he'd only play two matches.
I expected more from it, but it's the way it is and it's the way we had to act.
He wants to be there, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will be abled to improve Nick Faldo's record on Sunday for highest number of Ryder Cup matches played.
It may also mean the end of a Ryder Cup career in which he has never quite matched the highlights of his personal career.
Mickelson has won 18 matches, 20 lost matches and 7 draws to his name, but Furyk said his presence had a certain intangible influence on the team.
"He's funny, he's sarcastic, he's caught, he likes to make jokes with people and he's a great guy to have on the team" he explained.
"I think the younger players liked to fool him every once in a while last week, which was funny to watch.
“He has so much more to offer than his game.”
Thomas Bjorn, captain of the European team, knows that big lead can disappear quickly
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of the European team, knows from experience that the comfortable lead prior to the single matches on the last day of the Ryder Cup, can easily turn into a less comfortable situation.
The Dane made his debut in the match in Valderrama in 1997, where the team with as captain Seve Ballesteros had a five-point lead over the Americans, but ultimately passed the finish line with the smallest possible margin: 141⁄2-131⁄2.
"You keep reminding yourself that we had a big lead in Valderrama; we had a big lead in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but just" Bjorn said, called off here, after he had seen the 2018 batch win 5-3 on Friday and yesterday, good for a 10-6 lead at Le Golf National.
So the history shows me and everyone else in the team that this is not yet a played match.
Everyone will have to be fully committed tomorrow.
Get started and execute the plan on the right one.
It's not over until the points are on the board.
We have a goal, and that's winning this trophy, and we need to keep focusing on that.
“I’ll focus on the 12 players we have, but we’re also aware of the people on the other side, some of the best players in the world.”
Rejoiced about the performance of his players on a tricky golf course, Bjorn added: "I would never prejudge business in this area.
A new challenge awaits tomorrow.
Tomorrow the individual achievements will be discussed, and that is a completely different discipline.
It's great to be going out there with a partner if things are going well, but when you're on your own, your abilities as a golfer will only really be tested.
That's the message you have to tell the players, try to get the best out of yourself tomorrow.
So now let your partner go and he also has to get the best out of himself.""
Unlike Bjorn, his opponent, Jim Furyk, expects his players individually to perform better than as partners. The exceptions are Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas who captured three of the four points.
Furyk himself has experience with both sides of this kind of turnaround on the last day, as a member of the winning team in Brookline and as a loser when Europe realised the 'Miracle of Medinah'.
"I remember every damn word of that speech" he said in response to the question how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, had boosted his players for the last day.
"We've got 12 important matches ahead tomorrow, but let's try to get off to a good start, like in Brookline, or like in Medinah.
When we have the momentum, there is a lot of pressure on the matches in the middle.
We've tuned our line-up to this and send the men on the road in the order we think is the best and, you know, we're going to try to conjure something out of the top hat tomorrow.""
Thomas has the task of trying to lead the counter-offensive and deploys Rory McIlroy for the first match, and Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter are the other Europeans in the first half of the battle order.
"I've chosen this group in this order, because I think we're so constantly covered" Bjorn said of his selection of single players.
Germany's new warship postponed again
The German Navy's latest frigate should have entered service in 2016, replacing the outdated warships from the time of the Cold War, but it will take at least another year for it to be ready, due to faulty systems and increasing costs, local media reported.
The commissioning of the 'Rheinland-Pfalz', the leading ship in the brand new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the news magazine Die Zeit quoting a military spokesman.
The craft should have been part of the navy in 2014, but the worrying issues after delivery have plagued the fate of the ambitious project.
The four vessels of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class ordered by the navy in 200, serve as a replacement for the obsolete frigates of the Bremen class.
As far as we know, they feature a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, and a form of stealth technology, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic characteristics.
Other important features are longer maintenance cycles, it should be possible to deploy the latest frigates for up to two years in a row, far away from home ports.
The continued delays, however, mean that the advanced warships, which are intended to give Germany the opportunity to project its power overseas, are already obsolete by the time they are put into operation, Die Zeit notes.
The fateful frigate F125 already made headlines last year, when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into operation and sent it back to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy sent a ship back to a shipbuilder after delivery.
Little is known about the reasons behind this return, but German media cited a number of crucial 'software and hardware defects' that would make the warship useless as it would be deployed in a combat mission.
Software defects were especially important because the vessels of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class are operated by a crew of about 120 sailors, half the manpower on the frigates of the older Bremen class.
Also it became clear that the ship suffers from a severe overweight, which will reduce performance and limit the navy's capabilities to carry out further upgrades.
The 'Rheinland-Pfalz' weighs 7,000 tons, which is twice as much as ships of a similar class deployed by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to the faulty hardware, the price tag for the entire project, including the training of the crew, also becomes a problem.
It is said to have reached a staggering €3.1 billion, compared to the original €2.2 billion.
The problems the latest frigates have in their grip are becoming particularly important because of the recent warnings that the German navy's power is diminishing.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the parliamentary defence committee in Germany, acknowledged that the navy has actually "a shortage of deployable ships."
The official said that the problem has grown over time like a snowball, as old ships were taken out of operation but no replacement vessels were delivered.
He complained that none of the frigates of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class were yet available to be included in the navy.
National Trust eavesdrops on the secret life of bats
New research being carried out on an estate in the Scottish highlands, trying to reveal how bats make use of the landscape in their search for food.
The hope is that the findings will shine new light on the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and contribute to future conservation activities.
The study carried out by scholars from the National Trust for Scotland follows the common and small dwarf bat, the brown big-eared bat and the water bat at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recording equipment will be placed at key venues around the estate to track the activities of the bats throughout the season.
Employees of the NTS and volunteers will also carry out mobile investigations with the help of hand detectors.
Expert sound analysis of all recordings will determine the frequency of the crites of the bat and which kind does what.
Next, a map of the habitat and a report is produced in order to create a detailed image on a landscape scale of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature conservation adviser to the NTS, hopes the results will show which areas of the habitat are the most important for bats and how they are used by the species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management, such as making pasture fields and how we can best maintain the forests for bats and similar species.
Bat populations in Scotland and in the rest of the UK have declined significantly over the past century.
They are threatened by construction and development activities that affect berths and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lightnings can also pose a risk, as well as flycatchers and certain chemical treatments of building materials and attacks by domestic cats.
In reality, bats are not blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to catching prey.
They use an advanced ultrasound location technique to determine the position of insects and obstacles in their flight path.
At the NTS, which is responsible for caring about more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of ashore in the country, bats are taken very seriously.
The NTS has ten trained experts, who regularly carry out inspections of berths and sometimes rescue operations.
The organisation has even set up the first and only reserve for bats in Scotland at the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, where eight of Scotland's ten species of bakes live.
Landscape manager David Thompson says the estate is the ideal territory for the bats.
"Here in Threave we have a great environment for bats" he said.
"We have the old buildings, a lot of very old trees and the right habitat.
But there is so much not yet known about bats, and that’s why the work we’re doing here and in other locations will help us better understand what they need to thrive.”
He emphasizes the importance of the control of bats before maintenance work is carried out, as the potentially unconscious destruction of a sleeping place could lead to the death of 400 female bats and their young, potentially exterminating an entire local population.
Bats are a protected species and it is illegal to kill, harass or disturb them or to destroy their sleeping places.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Schot's officer for the Bat Conservation Trust, has asked the public to make a contribution to help.
She said: "We still have to learn a lot about our bats and from many of our species we simply don't know yet how their populations are doing."
Ronaldo rejects charges of sexual assault as his lawyers get ready to prosecute a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo accusations of sexual assault qualified as 'fake news' and said people 'want to promote themselves' by using his name.
His lawyers are about to denounce the German weekly Der Spiegel, in which the allegations have been published.
Portugal's and Juventus strikers are accused of assaulting an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga, in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
In addition, he is accused of paying her $375,000 to remain silent about the incident, Der Spiegel reported on Friday.
Through a video for his 142 million followers on Instagram Live, Ronaldo, 33, mentioned the reports 'fake news' several hours after the allegations were reported.
"No, no, no, no.
What they're saying today, fake news," the winner of five Golden Balls says on camera.
"They want to promote themselves by mentioning my name.
- That's normal.
They want to become famous by mentioning my name, but that's part of the profession.
I'm a happy man, and through and through good" the footballer added, smiling.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing a lawsuit in which they intend to prosecute Der Spiegel for the accusations they called "an inadmissible report of privacy suspicions," according to Reuters.
Christian Schertz, lawyer, said the footballer wants compensation for the 'moral damages for an amount corresponding to the seriousness of the infringement, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years'.
The alleged incident reportedly took place in June 2009 in a suite of the Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
Having met at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga, according to the report, have gone back to the footballer's room, where, according to the accusation, he raped her anally, as can be read in the charges filed with the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims Ronaldo fell to his knees after the alleged incident and that he told her he was a 'good guy' for '99 percent', but that he is sometimes abandoned that 'one percent'.
The documents state that Ronaldo confirms that the couple has had sex, but that this was with mutual agreement.
Mayorga also alleges that she went to the police and that there have been photos taken of her injuries in a hospital, but that she later agreed to an extrajudicial arrangement because she was 'afraid of the consequences' and because she was worried about the 'public humiliation'.
The 34-year-old now says she wants to have the scheme invalidated because she is permanently traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was about to go from Manchester United to Real Madrid at the time of the alleged sexual assault and left for Italian giant Juventus this summer in a deal worth €100 million.
Brex: The UK 'will regret it forever' if car manufacturers leave
The UK 'will regret it forever' if it loses its status as a world leader in car production after Brexit, said Greg Clark, economic minister.
He added it was 'worrisome' that Toyota UK had said to the BBC that if Britain left the EU without a deal, the company would temporarily halt production at Burnaston plant, near Derby.
"We need a deal," Clark said.
Japan's car producer said the impact of delays at the border in the event of a 'no-deal Brexit' was going to cost jobs.
The Burnaston plant, where Toyota's Auris and Avensis are made, produced nearly 150,000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to other countries across the European Union.
"My vision is that if Britain crashes out of the EU at the end of March, we'll see production at our factory shut down," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's managing director at Burnaston.
Other car products in the UK, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also expressed their fears about the consequences of leaving the EU without an agreement for the functioning of cross-border trade.
For example, BMW has already hinted that it will close the Mini factory in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
Most of the concerns the car manufacturers are concerned about risks in the supply chain in the event of a 'no-deal Brexit'.
Toyota's production line runs based on the 'just-in-time' principle, with every 37-minute parts arriving from suppliers in the UK and in the EU for cars made to order.
If the UK leaves the EU without a deal on March 29, unrest could emerge at borders that, according to the industry, could lead to delays and shortages of parts.
Toyotoy cannot store more than one day of stock at the Derbyshire plant, the company said, which is why production would be halted in that case.
Clark said Theresa May's Chequers plan for the future relationship with the EU is 'exactly aligned to avoid those checks at the border'.
"We have to have a deal. We want to have the best deal, which will not only allow us to continue the current success, but also seize this opportunity" he told the listeners of BBC Radio 4's Today programme.
"The proof, not only from Toyota but also from other producers, is that we absolutely must be able to continue with this very successful set of supply chains."
Toyota could not say how long production would be halted, but warned in the longer term that the additional costs would affect the company's competitive position and that this would eventually cost jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working at Burnaston for 24 years, and who is a representative for the trade union Unite at the factory, said his members are increasingly worried: "In my experience, once these jobs have been lost, they will never come back.
A government spokesman said: "We have submitted a precise and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU."
Trump and Rosenstein's meeting could be delayed again, the White House says
The crucial meeting between Donald Trump and Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could 'be postponed for another week' as the fight over the High Court candidate, Brett Kavanaugh, continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Counsel Robert Mueller, who is investigating Russia's influencing the election, links between Trump and Russia's aides and possible impediment to the president's course of justice.
The question of whether Trump is going to fire the deputy attorney general and thereby jeopardize Mueller's independence will keep the Washington gossip machine going for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein had discussed the possibility of wearing a microphone to capture conversations with Trump and to impeach the president through the 25th amendment.
Rosenstein denied this report.
But when he went to the White House last Monday, rumors circulated that he was about to file his resignation.
Instead, a meeting with Trump, who was then at the United Nations in New York, was announced for Thursday.
Trump said he 'preferably didn't' want to fire Rosenstein, but then the meeting was moved to avoid a conflict with a Senate Justice Committee hearing, in which Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual misbehavior, Dr Christine Blasey Ford, would both testify.
On Friday, Trump gave the FBI orders to conduct a one-week investigation into accusations against Kavanaugh, delaying voting in the full Senate again.
On Sunday, Sarah Sanders, Trump's press secretary appeared on Fox News.
Asked about the meeting with Rosenstein she said: "No date has yet been set for that, it could be this week, but it could also be postponed for another week, given all the other things going on with the Supreme Court.
But we'll see and I'd always like to keep the press informed.""
Some reporters struggled with this assertion: Sanders hasn't held a press conference at the White House since September 10.
The host Chris Wallace asked her why.
Sanders said the scarcity of press conferences wasn't triggered by an aversion to the 'attention-grabbing' TV journalists, though, she added: "I won't deny that they're trying to attract attention."
Subsequently, she suggested that direct contact between Trump and the press will increase.
"The president does more question-and-answer sessions than any president before him," she said, without providing evidence for this: "We've looked at the numbers."
Press conferences will still take place, Sanders said, but "if the press has the chance to ask the president of the United States directly, that's infinitely better than talking to me.
We're trying to do that a lot and you've seen us do that many times over the last few weeks and that's going to take the place of a press conference when you can talk to the president of the United States."
Trump regularly answers questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in public sessions or during press conferences with dignitaries visiting.
Press conferences with only the president are rare.
In New York this week, the president possibly demonstrated why, he made a candid and at times bizarre appearance for the assembled reporters.
Minister for Health Care Writes a Letter on Brexit to NHS Scotland workers from other EU countries
The health minister has written a letter to the EU staff working in the NHS Scotland to express the country's gratitude and the desire that they continue to work at the NHS after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, MSP, sent a letter with less than six months to go before the UK withdraws from the EU.
The Scottish government has already expressed its willingness to bear the costs of applying for permanent status for EU citizens working for the decentralised public services.
In her letter, Freeman wrote: "During the summer, negotiations between the UK and the EU have continued, and expected decisions will be taken this fall.
But at the same time, the UK government has also ramped up preparations for a possible scenario in which no deal is reached.
I know these must be insecure times for all of you.
That is why I would like to emphasise once again how much I appreciate the contribution of all staff, irrespective of their nationality.
Colleagues from across the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and enhance the work of the NHS and that provide benefits to the patients and the communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home and we would like you to stay here."
Christion Abercrombie is undergoing emergency surgery after suffering head injuries
The Tennessee State Tigers line baker, Christion Abercrombie, underwent emergency surgery yesterday after suffering a head injury in the 31-27-lost match against the Vanderbilt Commodores, Mike Organ of the Tennessean reported.
Rod Reed, Tennessee State's head coach, told reporters that the accident occurred shortly before halftime.
"He came to the sidelines and slumped more or less there," Reed said.
The trainers and the medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen directly on the sidelines, before putting him on a stretcher and taking him for further investigation.
A Tennessee State official told Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee, that Abercrombie had come out of the operating room at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that 'no details yet are known about the type/size of the injury' and Tennessee State is trying to invest when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a 'redshirt sophomore', is in his first season at Tennessee State after a transfer from Illinois.
He has been tackled a total of five times on Saturday before he left the field, prompting him to be tackled a total of 18 times this season.
Foreign buyers will have to pay higher fees when they buy a home in the UK
Foreign buyers will have to pay higher fees when they buy a home in the U.K. and the proceeds will be used to help the homeless according to new plans from the Conservatives
This action will have to neutralize Corbyn's success to attract young voters
The increase in fees is levied on individuals who don't pay taxes in the UK
The finance ministry expects this to raise £120 million a year, to help the homeless
Foreign buyers will have to pay higher fees when they buy a home in the UK and the additional proceeds will be used to help the homeless, Theresa May announced today.
This action is seen as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn's success that attracts young voters with promises to ensure more affordable homes and recount this on many earners.
The increase in fees will be levied on individuals and firms that do not pay taxes in the UK, with the additional proceeds invested in an attempt by the government to combat homelessness.
The surcharge, which comes on top of the current fees, including the higher levels introduced two years ago for second homes and homes being purchased for the rental, could amount to up to three percent.
The Ministry of Finance expects to raise to £120 million a year in this way.
An estimated 13 per cent of new construction in London is purchased by individuals who do not live in the UK. As a result, prices are increased and it is difficult for starters to get a foot on the house ladder.
Many affluent areas in the country, particularly in the capital, have become practically 'ghost towns' because of the high number of foreign buyers who spend the most time outside the country.
This new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction in fees to help young people buy their first home.
He accused major construction firms of keeping prices high by buying up land and not using it and calling on May to abolish quotas on affordable homes in order to restore the 'housing shame' in Britain.
Corbyn has announced a striking series of housing reforms, including rental ceilings and an end to eviction 'without negligence'.
He also wants to give local authorities more opportunities to build new homes.
May said: "Last year, I said I wanted to spend my time as prime minister recovering the 'British Dream,' that life is getting better for every new generation.
And that means that our faulty housing market has to be restored.
Great Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build a life here.
However, it can't be that it's just as simple for individuals who don't live in the UK, and for foreign companies, to buy houses here as it is for hard-working British residents.
For too many people, the dream of owning a house once became too vague a dream and the humiliation of life without a roof over his head has become too real."
Jack Ross: 'My ultimate ambition is managing Scotland'
Jack Ross, Sunderland's boss, says it's his 'ultimate ambition' to one day become manager of Scotland.
The Scot, 42, enjoys the challenge of reviving the club from the north-east of England, which is currently ranked third in League One, on three points from first spot.
He went to the Stadium of Light last summer after the previous season St Mirren had returned to the Scottish Premiership.
"As a footballer, I wanted to play for my homeland.
But I was selected for the B-team and with that the stocking was off" Ross told Sportsound on BBC Scotland.
"When I was a kid I saw together with my dad Scotland often playing in Hampden Park and it's something that keeps attracting me.
However, that opportunity wouldn’t come until I’m successful in managing a club.”
Ross's predecessors as Sunderland managers include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Alloa Athletic's former boss says he was not intimidated to become the successor to these well-established names at a big club, previously he had denied advances from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"Success for me is currently being measured by 'can I have this club return to the Premier League?'
Because of the structure and the facilities at this club, it should undoubtedly be in the Premier League," he says.
"It's not an easy task to achieve this, but I can't see myself as successful until I bring the club back there."
Ross has only been engaged in his career as a manager for three years, after a period as an assistant manager at Dumbarton and 15 months in Hearts' coaching group.
Subsequently, he helped Alloa recover from relegation to the third division and has transformed St Mirren from on the verge of relegation to winning the Championship in the following season.
And Ross says he now feels more comfortable than he ever did during his career as a footballer at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It was probably really a crossroads" he recalled from the moment he took charge at Alloa.
"I really believed that management was the right choice for me, much more than playing.
It sounds bizarre, because I did quite okay, I earned reasonably well and knew a few reasonable highlights.
But playing football can be tough.
There are a lot of things you are confronted with on a weekly basis.
I still have that, given the stress and pressure of the profession, but management just feels good.
I've always wanted to be a manager and now that it's real to me, I'm as good as I've never felt in my adult life.""
You can listen to the full interview at Sportsound on Sunday, 30 September, at Radio Scotland between 12:00 and 13:00 BST
The perfect time for a pint is 17:30 on a Saturday, research reveals
The heatwave in the summer has raised revenue from British pubs struggling to survive, but has also put more pressure on restaurant chains.
Groups with pubs and bars saw sales rise by 2.7 percent in July, but revenues in restaurants were 4.8 percent less, the figures show.
Peter Martin, from the corporate consulting firm CGA, which collected the figures, said: "Continuous sunshine and the fact that England competed longer than expected in the world championships, meant that July showed a similar pattern as June, when the pubs had 2.8 percent more turnover, with that difference that the restaurants were now hit even harder.
The fall of 1.8 per cent in the turnover of restaurants in June only got worse in July.
Pubs and bars focused on liquor sales outperformed by far the best, with better LFL (like-for-like) growth (like-for-like) than restaurants going down.
Pubs that are also on the sale of food members also from the sun, but not as dramatically as the restaurants.
Looks like people were just going out for drinking.
In pubs and bars under management, liquor turnover was 6.6 percent higher for the month, while food sales were 3 percent lower."
Paul Newman of RSM, analysts for the leisure and hospitality industry, said: "These results continue the trend we've been seeing since the end of April.
The weather and impact of important social or sporting events remain the biggest factors for the turnover in the entertainment market.
It is therefore no surprise that restaurant groups continue to struggle, even though sales of 4.8 percent year-on-year is particularly painful given the ongoing cost pressure.
The long, hot summer couldn’t have come at a worse time for the operators focusing on food and only time will teach whether August’s more temperate temperatures can give the necessary respite.”
Total sales growth in the pub and restaurant industry, including new openings, was 2.7 percent in July, reflecting the decline of the introduction of new brands.
In the Coffer Peach Tracker, the revenue monitor for the pub, bar and restaurant sector in the UK, the performance data is collected and analysed from 47 operational groups, with a combined turnover of £9 billion. This is the established benchmark of the industry.
One in five children has a secret social media account, which they keep hidden from their parents
One in five children, a few no older than 11, have a secret account for social media, which they keep hidden from parents and teachers, a survey reveals
Surveillance among 20,000 pupils from secondary schools revealed growth in 'false Insta pages'
This news has intensified the fear that sexual content will be posted
Twenty percent of pupils say they have a 'main account' that they also show their parents
One in five children, a few not older than 11 years old, create social media accounts that they hide from adults.
A survey of 20,000 pupils from high schools unveiled rapid growth in 'false Insta accounts', a reference to Instagram, the photo sharing site.
This news has amplified the fear that sexual content will be posted.
Twenty percent of pupils say they are using a clean 'main account' that they show to their parents, while they also still have personal accounts.
A mother who accidentally ended up on her 13-year-old daughter's secret site discovered a teener who asked others to "rape" her.
The research, carried out by Digital Awareness UK and HMC (Headmasters" and Headmistresses' Conference) of independent schools, found that 40 percent of 11- to 18-year-olds had two profiles and half admitted to holding personal accounts.
Mike Buchanan, head of HCM, said: "It's disturbing that so many teenagers are lured to create online spaces where they are not found by their parents and teachers."
Eilidh Doyle becomes the 'voice of the athlete' on the Scottish Council for Athletics
Eilidh Doyle has been elected to the board of the Scottish athletics world, as non-executive director at the board's general annual meeting.
Doyle is the most-decorated athlete in Scotland on track and in the field and chairman Ian Beattie described her election as a great opportunity for the sport's directors to learn from her extensive experience at an international level over the past decade.
"Eilidh enjoys a lot of respect in Scotland's athletics community, the UK and the world and we are sure that the athletics in Scotland will benefit enormously from her election in the council" Beattie said.
Doyle said: "I am happy to be able to serve as a voice for the athletes and I hope that I can make a contribution and can help run the sport in Scotland."
The American who won the 200 meters and the 400 meters during the 1996 Games in Atlanta, two of his four gold Olympic medals, and who now regularly comments on the BBC, could no longer walk after a TIA.
He wrote on Twitter: "Today a month ago I had a TIA.
- I couldn't walk anymore.
The doctors told me that only time will tell if I will recover and to what extent.
It's been terribly hard work, but I've fully recovered now, I've learned to walk again and do my flexibility exercises again today!
Thank you for all the messages of support!"
Advertisement for breast pump comparing mothers to cows distributes online opinion
A company that makes breast pumps has divided the online opinion with an advertisement comparing suckling mothers to cows being milked.
To highlight the introduction of what is called “the world’s quietest portable breast pump,” Elvie, producer of consumer technology, has released an ironic music-inspired ad to illustrate the freedom that this new pump has to offer colving mothers.
Four real mothers dance in a hay-filled barn on a song that contains lyrics like: 'Yes, I milk myself, but you can't see any of it' and 'In case you didn't notice, these aren't udders, these are my boobs'.
The chorus then continues with: 'Pumping, pumping, pumping, I feed the babies, pumps, pumps, pumps, I milk the lady's'.
However, the advertisement, which was published on the company's Facebook page, directly caused an online controversy.
The video has been viewed 77,000 times and has provoked hundreds of comments, especially mixed reactions from viewers, in which many people say that it condones the 'horror' of the dairy industry.
"It's a bad choice to use cows to advertise this product.
Just like us, they have to get into expectation and give birth to produce milk, but in their case the babies are taken away within days of childbirth" is written in a response.
Elvie's breast pump fits discreetly in a nursing bra (Elvie/Mother).
Someone else commented: "This, of course, is traumatic for mother and baby.
But yes, why don’t we use them to advertise a breast pump for mothers who are allowed to keep their babies?”
Anyone else added: "This ad is so far from reality."
Others defended the ad and a woman admitted she found the song "hilarious."
"I think this is a brilliant idea.
I'd get one if I was still breastfeeding.
That pumping gave me the feeling of being a cow.
The ad's a bit weird, but I haven't searched too much behind it.
This is a brilliant product" someone wrote.
Someone else had the following comment: "This is a funny ad, aimed at mammas pumping (often in the workplace or in a toilet) and then feeling like 'cows'.
This advertisement does not speak positively or negatively about the dairy industry."
At the end of the video, you'll see the group of women all dancing with the pump discreetly hidden in their bras.
The concept behind the campaign is based on the understanding that many women who pump sometimes feel like cows.
The Elvie Pump, however, is completely silent, has no wires or snakes and fits discreetly in a nursing bra so that women have the freedom to move, hold their babies and even go out while they are pumping.
Ana Balarin, partner and ECD at Mother says: "The Elvie Pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a tough and provocative introduction.
By making a comparison between pumping mothers and dairy cows, we wanted to place the pumping and the associated challenges in the spotlight and at the same time show in an entertaining and understandable way how the new pump will create an incredible sense of freedom.
This isn't the first time Elvie's pump pulls the front page.
During London Fashion Week, a two-year-old mother appeared on Marta Jakubowski's catwalk while using the product.
Hundreds of migrant children tacitly moved to a tent camp on the Texas border
The number of detained migrant children has peaked, although the number of monthly border crossings has remained relatively unchanged, in part because the harshy rhetoric and harsh policies introduced by the Trump administration are making it more difficult to accommodate children among sponsors.
Traditionally, most sponsors themselves are undocumented immigrants and are afraid to endanger their own opportunity to stay in the country by signing up for a child.
That risk has increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential sponsors and the remaining adult members of their household will have to hand over their fingerprints and that this data will be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week, Matthew Albence, a senior Immigration and Customs Enforcement official, stated in testimony before congress that his organization has arrested dozens of people who signed up as sponsors for unaccompanied minors.
The organization later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested did not have a prior criminal record.
"Nearly 80 percent of the individuals who are sponsors or relatives of the sponsors are illegal in this country, and a large part of them are criminal aliens.
We are prosecuting these individuals," Mr. Albence said.
In an attempt to process the children more quickly, officials have introduced new rules, requiring some of them to appear in court within one monthrap after the start of detention, rather than within 60 days, which was previously the standard, according to employees at the shelter
Many of them will stand in court through a video conferencing, rather than in person, to advocate their application for legal status before an immigration judge.
Those deemed not to be eligible for assistance will be deported quickly.
The longer the children remain in custody, the more likely they are to become anxious or depressed, which can give rise to violent outbursts or flight attempts, according to the employees at the shelter and according to reports that have come out of the system in recent months.
Lawyers said these concerns are stronger at a larger facility, such as Tornillo, where the signs that a child is wrestling are more often ignored, due to the size of the camp.
They added that moving the children to the tent camp without giving them enough time to prepare for this emotionally or to say goodbye to friends can contribute to the trauma that many of them are already struggling with anyway.
Syria orders U.S., France and Turkey's 'occupancy force' to withdraw immediately
In a speech to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid Al-Muallem also urged the Syrian refugees to return, even now that the war in the country is in its eighth year.
Moualem, who is also Deputy Prime Minister, said the foreign troops are illegally on Syrian soil, under the pretense of fighting terrorism and that 'this will be handled accordingly'.
"They must withdraw immediately and without any conditions" he told the Assembly.
Moualem says the "war on terror is almost over" in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have died since 2011 and millions more have been uprooted and expelled from their homes.
He said Damascus would proceed with "this holy battle until we have liberated all Syrian territories" from terror groups and from a "possibly illegal foreign presence."
The United States has about 2,000 troops in Syria, who are mainly engaged in training and advising the Kurdish forces and the Syrian Arabs who are battling President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1,000 troops in the war-torn country.
With regard to the refugees, Moualem said the conditions are favorable for their return and he accused 'single Western countries spreading irrational fears' leaving most refugees away.
"We have appealed to the international community and humanitarian organizations to facilitate this return" he said.
"They're politicizing a case that should only be a humanitarian problem."
The United States and the European Union have warned that there will be no reconstruction aid available for Syria, until a political agreement is concluded between Assad and the opposition to end the war.
United Nations diplomats say a recent agreement between Russia and Turkey to establish a buffer zone in Idlib, the rebels' last major stronghold, has created the opportunity to start political talks.
The deal between Russia and Turkey has averted a large-ranging attack from Russian-backed Syrian forces on the province, where three million people live.
Moualem, however, stressed that this agreement has 'clear deadlines' and said he hoped the military action will target jihadists, including fighters from Al-Qaeda-linked Nusra Front, who 'will be exterminated.'
Staffan de Mistura, U.N. delegate, soon hopes to convene the first meeting of a new committee made up of members of the government and the opportunity to draft a post-war constitution for Syria and pave the way for elections.
Moualem made it clear what the conditions of the Syrian government are to participate in the committee and said the panel's work should limit itself 'to revising the articles of the current constitution' and he warned against outside interference.
What Trump will win a second term
According to that logic, Mr. Trump would win the 2020 election unless, as many 'liberal' viewers probably hope, his presidency is prematurely terminated by impeachment proceedings and scandals.
In what would undoubtedly be 'the most dramatic final of a presidency ever'.
At the moment, however, there are no signs of viewing fatigue.
Since 2014, the ratings during 'prime-time' at CNN have more than doubled to 1.05 million and nearly tripled to 1.6 million at MSNBC.
Fox News has an average of 2.4 million viewers during 'prime-time', compared to 1.7 million viewers four years ago, and 'The Rachel Maddow Show' by MSNBC is at the top of the ratings for cable shows with 3.5 million viewers on evenings with important news.
"This is a fire attracting people, because it's something we don't understand" said Neal Baer, the person responsible behind the ABC-drama 'Designated Survivor' a series about a minister becoming president after an attack destroyed the Capitol.
Nell Scovell, an experienced creator of comedy and author of "Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys Club" has a different theory.
She recalls a taxi ride in Boston before the 2016 election.
The driver told her he was going to vote for Trump.
- Why? she asked.
"He then said 'Because he makes me laugh,"" Scovell told me.
There's entertainment value in the chaos.
Of course, unlike everything else on TV, the storylines that come out of Washington can influence the future of Roe v. Wade or determine whether immigrant families can reunite or affect the status of the global economy.
Disabling is a luxury that only most privileged viewers can afford.
And yet, it goes beyond wanting to be a well-informed citizen, when you notice that you've been watching for six hours watching a panel of experts discussing the use of sources from the 'deep background' by Bob Woodward in his book 'Fear', who are talking about Paul Manafort's coat of peacock leather that has cost $15,000 ('a garment full of overconfidence' according to The Washington Post
At least I'll never be able to watch Super Mario in the same way again.
"What he's doing to give you the idea that it's a reality show is partly that he's tucking something new to you every night," Brent Montgomery, CEO of Wheelhouse Entertainment and the creator of 'Pawn Stars', said about the rotating role division of the Trump show and the day-to-day plot changes (filing into a fight with the N.F.L., Kim Jong-un praise).
You can't afford to miss an episode or you feel like you're falling behind.
When I reached Fleiss earlier this week, it was sunny and 26 degrees outside his house on the north coast of Kauai, but he was sitting inside watching MSNBC while recording CNN.
He couldn't rip himself off the screen, with Brett Kavanaugh having to appear before the Senate Judicial Committee and the future of the Supreme Court that was at stake.
"I remember that we were doing all those weird shows, not so long ago, and that the people said 'this is the beginning of the end of Western civilisation,'" Fleiss told me.
"I thought it was a joke, but now it turns out they were right."
Amy Chozick, a writer who makes irregular contributions to The Times about business, politics and the media, is the author of the "Chasing Hillary" memoir.
Money from the outside flows into the most exciting midterm elections to the House of Representatives
Unsurprisingly, Pennsylvania's 17th district sees an influx of money, thanks to a recast of a constituency through which two incumbent parliamentarians have to compete for the same seat.
Pittsburg's recently rearranged district affects Democratic Representative Conor Lamb, who has acquired his seat in another district in a special election last spring.
Lamb must compete against another incumbent, the Republican Keith Rothfus, who is currently representing the old 12th district of Pennsylvania, who has a lot of overlap with the new 17th district.
The cards have been reclassified after the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled in January that the old districts were unconstitutionally classified ('gerrymandering') in favor of the Republicans.
The race in the new 17th district has led to a fighting match on campaign funding between the financial arms of the two major parties, the DCCC (Democratic Campaign Congressional Committee) and the NRCC (National Republican Campaign Committee).
Lamb became a well-known name in Pennsylvania after a narrow victory in a well-watched special election for Pennsylvania's 18th constituency.
This seat had been occupied by a Republican for more than a decade, and President Donald Trump had conquered this district with 20 percentage points difference.
According to political experts, the Democrats have a small advantage.
The U.S
Diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama Beijing had already acknowledged and received little negative response from Washington.
Trump had a heartfelt meeting with President Juan Carlos Varela of Panama in June 2017) and he owned a hotel in Panama until his partners kicked out the Trump Organization's management team.
Officials of the United States Foreign Office have decided to recall U.S. heads of diplomatic missions from El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama over the 'recent decision to no longer recognize Taiwan,' Heather Nauert, the ministry's spokeswoman, said in a statement earlier this month.
But only penalties were considered in the case of El Salvador, as the country received $140 million in U.S. aid in 2017, for control of narcotics, development and economic support.
The proposed penalties, including reductions in financial support and targeted visa restrictions would have become painful for the Central American country with high unemployment and high homicide rates.
As the internal meetings continued, North American and Central American officials postponed a high-leveling conference. This would focus on security and economic prosperity as follow-up at a similar gathering last year, which was seen as a step forward in the attempts to prevent migrants from leaving for the United States.
But in mid-Septembery, a number of government officials hinted that they wanted the conference to go ahead, making all possible punitive measures against El Salvador impossible in advance.
The plan now is that Vice President Mike Pence will deliver a speech at the conference, which is now scheduled for mid-October, as a sign of the government's importance assigning to the gathering, diplomats say.
And the three U.S. envoys have returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic, with no harsh messages about Washington's punitive measures.
A White House spokesman on behalf of Mr. Bolton declined to comment on the details of the debate described by three U.S
Their reports were confirmed by an external analyst who is also close to the government and who also spoke on condition of anonymity.
Examine history
The next step may be the Special Counsel Robert Mueller's report on Trump's possible opposition, for which major evidence has now become public.
Mueller is also likely to focus his research on collusion between Trump's and Russia's campaign in an attack by Russia on our elections.
If Congress changes colour, Trump has to answer for that institution, just as he's getting ready to turn back to voters, and maybe he'll have to answer to a jury in a court.
Those are a lot of lifes, and I don't want to suggest that Trump's fall is inevitable, nor that of his equivalents in Europe.
There will have to be choices made by all of us, on both sides of the Atlantic, who will determine how long the battle will last.
In 19383, German officers were ready to carry out a 'coup d'état' against Hitler, but only if the West had resisted and supported Czechoslovakia in Munich.
As a result, we failed and missed an opportunity to prevent the years of bleading that followed.
The course of history revolves around such bending points and the inexorable advance of democracy is thereby accelerated or delayed.
Americans are currently confronted with several of this kind of inflection points.
What will we do if Trump fires Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who holds the fate of Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein has been in a difficult position since the moment this magazine reported that, last year, he had suggested to record conversations with the president in secret and that he had speculated about the president's suitability for office.
Rosenstein says The Times's report doesn't add up.
"How shall we respond if the FBI's new requested investigation into Brett Kavanaugh is not complete or unfair, or if he is appointed to the Supreme Court despite credible accusations of sexual assault and unfair testimony?
And above all, will we vote in the midterm elections for a Congress that will hold Trump to account?
If we fail in these tests, democracy awaits a very long winter.
But I don't believe we're going to fail because of the lesson I've learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czech Jewer who was deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazia regime that had once occupied my ambassador's residence.
She survived, immigrated to America and, 60 years later, sent me away to light candles for the Sabbath on that table on which a swastika is depicted.
With that as my legacy, how can I not be optimistic about our future?"
Norman Eisen, a senior fellow of the Brookings Institution, is the chairman of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and the author of 'The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House.'
Graham Dorrans of Glasgow Rangers is optimistic about the match against Rapid Wien
Rangers get Rapid Wien visiting on Thursday and know that a win over the Austrians, after the impressive draw in Spain against Villarreal earlier this month, puts them in a strong position to qualify in Group G of the Europa League.
Due to a knee injury, midfielder Graham Dorrans could not play his first game of the season until the 2-2 draw at Villarreal, but he believes the Rangers can use that result as a springboard to better results.
"It was a good point for us because Villarreal really have a good team" the 31-year-old said.
"We entered the match with the idea that there was something to win and came out of the match with a point.
Maybe we could even have won in the end, but over the whole match, a draw is probably a fair result.
They were probably better in the first half but when we came out in the second half we were the better team.
Next Thursday will be another big European evening.
Hopefully we can get three points but it's going to be a difficult match because they also had a good result in their last game. But if the audience stands behind us, I'm sure that we can push through and that we can achieve a positive result.
Last year has certainly been tough, of everything that happened with my injuries and the changes in the club itself, but now there's a feel-good factor on the club.
The team is good and the boys really enjoy it. The training is good.
Hopefully we can push through now, leave last season and become successful again.""
Women can't sleep because of fear of retirement
Despite the fact that the participants in the survey had a clear idea of the way they want to receive care, few people discuss this with their relatives.
About halvare participants in Nationwide's study said they are talking to their spouses about the cost of long-term care.
And only 10 percent said they had talked to their children about this.
"People would like a family member to take care of them, but they don't take the steps necessary to have this conversation" said Holly Snyder, vice president for life insurance at Nationwide.
This is where we're gonna start.
Talk to your spouse and the children: You can’t prepare your family to take care if you don’t express your wishes properly on time.
Collaborate with your advisor and your family to discuss where and how you’re going to receive care, as these choices can be a significant factor in determining costs.
Hire a financial advisor: Your advisor can also give you advice on how to pay for these expenses.
Your long-term financing choices, among other things, can be a traditional long-term care insurance policy, a hybrid life insurance policy with cash value to help cover these expenses, or wagering equity as insurance, as long as you have money.
Create your legal documents: Avoid a legal battle when insurance needs to be deployed.
Provide a power of attorney for health care so that you can designate a trusted person who can oversee your medical care and ensure that the professionals stick to your wishes in case you can no longer communicate.
Also consider a power of attorney for your financial affairs.
In that case, you select a trusted person to make financial decisions on your behalf and ensure that the invoices are paid if you can no longer do so yourself.
Don't forget the little details: Imagine your elderly father or mother hitting a medical emergency and is on your way to the hospital.
In that case, can you answer questions about medication and allergies?
Capture these details into a written plan so that you have the information at hand.
"It's not just the financial matters that matter, but who are the doctors?" asked Martin.
"Which medicines are used?
Who's taking care of the dog?
Make sure that plan comes true.”
Man shoted several times with air pressure rifle in Ilfracombe
A man has been shot several times with an air-pressure rifle as he walked home after a night out.
The victim, forty plus, found himself in the Oxford Grove near Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was hit in his chest, abdomen and hand.
Agents described the shelling, which took place around 02:30 BST, as a "random act."
The victim did not see who attacked him.
His injuries are not life-threatening and police have summoned witnesses to come forward.
Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed by a powerful earthquake and a tsunami the Indonesian city of Palu has hit on Friday, officials said, and the death toll is expected to rise further.
Because the means of communication have been disabled, the official authorities have not yet received information from the regency Donggala, an area north of Palu that is closer to the epicenter of the quake with a force of 7.5 on the Richter scale.
In Palu, more than 16,000 people have been evacuated after the disaster occurred.
Here are some important facts about Palu and Donggala, on the island of Celebes:
Palu is the capital of Central Celebes province and is located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of the island of Celebes, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city celebrated its 40th anniversary when the tsunami struck.
Donggala is a regency stretching more than 300 km along the coast of northwest Celebes.
The regency, an administrative region just below a province, had an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the main activities in the economy of Central Celebes province, particularly in the coastal area of Donggala.
Nickel mines are also important in the province, but for the most part they are located in Morowali, on the opposite coast of Celebes.
Palu and Donggala have been regularly affected by tsunamis over the past hundred years, according to Indonesia's anticipations Agency.
In 1938 a tsunami claimed more than 200 lives and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
A tsunami also struck in western Donggala in 1996, with nine deaths.
Indonesia is located on the Pacific Ring of Fire and is often affected by earthquakes.
Here’s a list of some of the heaviest earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A large earthquake on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh on North Sumatra on December 26 triggered a tsunami that affected 14 countries and claimed a total of 226,000 lives along the coasts of the Indian Ocean, more than half of that in Aceh.
2005: A series of major earthquakes occurred on the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people were killed on Nias Island, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: An Earthquake with a force of 6.8 on the Richter scale took place south of Java, Indonesia's most densely populated islance, causing a tsunami that struck the south coast and claimed nearly 700 lives.
2009: An Earthquake with a force of 7.6 on the Richter scale struck the city of Padang, capital of West Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: An earthquake with a force of 7.5 on the Richter scale struck one of the Mentawai Islands, off the coast of Sumatra, and triggered a tsunami of more than 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and demanded around 300 lifetimes.
2016: A shallow earthquake struck the regency of Pidie in Aceh and caused destruction and panic, as the people were reminded of the devastations of the deadly earthquake and tsunami of 2004.
There was no tsunami this time, but more than 100 people were killed by crashing buildings.
2018: Multiple earthquakes hit Indonesia's tourist island Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and caused thousands of tourists temporarily stranded.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on charges of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of Sarah Palin, the former governor of Alaska and candidate for the vice presidency, has been arrested on charges of domestic violence.
Palin, 29 from Wasilla, Alaska, had been arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, interference on a report of domestic violence and providing opposition during the judgment, according to a report released Saturday by the Alaska State Troopers.
According to the police's report, when a female acquaintance tried to call the police to report the alleged offences, he took the phone from her.
Palin is held in detention at Mat-Su home with a $500 bail, KTUU reported.
He appeared in court on Saturday and declared himself 'innocent, for sure' when asked to do so, the network reported.
Palin is indicted for three Class A offenses, meaning he can be sentenced to one year in prison and to a fine of up to $250,000.
It is also indicted for a Class B offense, punishable by a day in prison and a fine of $2,000.
It's not the first time Palin has been sued for offences.
In December 2017, he was accused of assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, then called police to report the alleged assault.
This case now serves before the veteran court of Alaska.
In January 2016, he was accused of an assault in the domestic sphere, interference on a report of domestic violence and possession of a weapon under the influence in connection with the incident.
His girlfriend claimed he had punched her in the face.
Sarah Palin was criticized in 2016 by a veteran group, having linked her son's violent behavior to PTSD, which he is said to have left over from his service in Iraq.
Indonesia, earthquake, tsunami: hundreds of deaths
At least 384 have died after an earthquake struck the Indonesian island of Celebes on Friday.
The quake with a force of 7.5 on the Richter scale has resulted in a tsunami and destroyed thousands of homes.
Electricity and communications networks are switched off while the death toll is expected to rise in the next few days.
The earthquake occurred just next to the center of Celebes, which is located northeast of Jakarta, the Indonesian capital.
Videos are circulating on social media showing the moment of impact.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a beach festival in the town of Palu when the tsunami reached the coast.
Federal prosecutors demand a rare death penalty for suspect of a terror attack in New York City
Federal prosecutors in New York are demanding the death penalty for Sajfoello Sajpov, the suspect of the New York City terror attack, in which eight persons have been killed, a rare sentence that has not been executed in this state since 1953.
Sajpov, 30, according to the indictment, used a rented truck from Home Depot to carry out an attack on a bike path along the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan, in which he knocked over pedestrians and cyclists who came his path in October.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors will have to prove that Sajpov 'deliberately' killed the eight victims and inflicted 'intentionally' serious bodily injuries, according to the letter of intent demanding the death penalty, which has been filed in New York's Southern District.
For both indictments, the death penalty is possible, according to the document submitted to the court.
Weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury approved the indictment with 22 charges, including eight charges of murder on behalf of a racket, typically an indictment used by federal prosecutors against organized crime, and an indictment of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required 'substantial planning and preparation' according to the prosecutors and they described the way in which Sajpov carried out the attack as 'horrific, cruel and depraved'.
"Sajfoello Habibulayevich Sajpov has inflicted injury, damage and loss on the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco" is stated in the statement of intent.
Five casualties were tourists from Argentina.
It's been more than a decade since in New York's Southern District a case that demanded the death penalty.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was sentenced for the murder of two drug suppliers but was eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time that the death penalty has been executed in a federal case in New York was in 1953 for Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a couple who have been executed after being convicted of spying for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
The Rosenbergs were both executed through the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Sajpov, from Uzbekistan, did not show remorse in the days and months after the attack, according to the court documents.
He let the detectives know that he was feeling good about his act, she the police.
Sajpov told authorities that he was inspired to carry out the attack after watching videos of ISIS on his phone, according to the indictment.
He also asked to hang the flag of ISIS in his room at the hospital, police recounted.
He pleaded not to be guilty of the indictment with 22 charges.
David Patton, one of the federal prosecutors representing Sajpov, said he's 'naturally disappointed' with prosecutors' decision.
“We think that the decision to demand the death penalty rather than accept an admission of guilt in exchange for a life imprisonment with no possibility of release will only prolong the trauma of these events for everyone involved,” Patton said.
Sajpov's defense had previously asked prosecutors not to demand the death penalty.
Conservative MP says NIGEL FARAGE should be in charge of Brexit negotiations
Nigel Farage pledged today to mobilize a 'people's army' during a protest at the Conservative Party conference.
Ukip's former leader said the politicians should 'feel heat' from the Eurosceptics, while one of Theresa May's own MPs suggested giving him the leadership of negotiations with the EU.
Conservative backbencher Peter Bone told participants at the march in Birmingham that the UK "would already be out" if Mr. Farage had been Brexit minister.
But the challenge for which May is faced in reconciling the strongly divided ranks is underscored by two Conservatives who are pro-Remain and who are taking part in a separate protest rally against Brexit in the city.
The prime minister is struggling to keep her Chequers compromise on track as this is under attack by Brexiteers, Remainers and the EU.
Supporters insisted that she will continue and will try to make a deal with Brussels despite the setbacks, thus forcing Eurosceptics and Labour to choose between her package and 'chaos'.
Bone said at a Leave Means Leafe meeting in Solihull that he wants to throw 'Chequers in the Trash.'
He suggested that Farage should be knighted and given responsibility for negotiations with Brussels.
"If he did the service, we'd be out now," Bone said.
MP for Wellingborough added: "I'll support Brexit, but we have to throw Chequers in the trash."
To clarify his opposition to the EU, he said: "We didn't fight in two world wars to be subservient.
“We want to create our own laws in our own country.”
Bone pointed out suggestions that public opinion had changed since the 2016 referendum: "The idea that the British people have changed views and want to remain in the EU is completely untrue."
Conservative Brexiteer Andrea Jenkyns also took part in the march and told reporters: "I'm just saying: Prime Minister, listen to the people.
Chequers is unpopular with the general public, the opposition is not going to vote, it is unpopular within our party and among our activists who go door-to-door and ensure that we are elected.
Drop Chequers and listen.""
In a focused message for May, she added: "Prime ministers keep their jobs, when they keep their promises."
Farage told the meeting that politicians should feel the 'heat' if they want to betray the decision taken in the 2016 referendum.
"This is now a matter of trust between us, the people, and our political class" he said.
"They're trying to betray Brexit and we're here together today to tell them 'we won't let you guys get away with this."
In a message to the enthusiastic masses, he added: "I want you to put our political class, which is about to betray Brexit, the fire.
We mobilize the people's army of this country that has given us victory in the Brexit referendum and we will never rest until we've once again become an independent, sovereign, proud United Kingdom.""
In the meantime, Remainers marched through Birmingham and concluded this march with a two-hour gathering in the city centre.
A small group of activists waved flags showing Tories Against Brexit, following the founding of this group over the weekend.
Lord Adonis, a member of the upper house for Labour, made jokes about the security concerns of a party app from the Conservatives at the start of their conference.
"These are the people telling us that they can take care of IT systems and all technology for Canada plus plus, for the frictionless boundary, for free trade without borders in Ireland," he added.
It's a full farce.
There's no such thing as a good Brexit," he added.
