President Donald Trump mocked Senator Dianne Feinstein at an election rally on Saturday that she insisted not to be the source of Christine Blasey Ford's leaked lecture, in which the Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh was charged with sexual assault.
Speaking at an election rally in Western Virginia, the president was not talking directly about the testimony given by Ford before the Senate Justice Committee. Instead, he hinted that what took place in the Senate showed that people can be “common, unkind and untruthful.”
"The only thing that can happen and the beautiful display that is taking place the last few days in the Senate, when you see the anger, when you are the people who are furious and mean and mean and unkind and untruthful" he said.
"When you look at the press releases and the leaks and that they then say 'oh, I didn't do it.
- I didn't do it.'
- Do you still remember?
Dianne Feinstein, did you leak?
Do you remember her answer... did you leak the document - 'oh, oh, what?
- Oh, no. No.
- I didn't leak."
- Well, wait a minute.
No, we didn't leak," he added, in an imitation of the senator.
In July, the letter mentioning accusations against Kavanaugh were sent to Feinstein by Ford and it was leaked in September, but Feinstein has denied her agency was the leak.
"I didn't hide Dr. Ford's accusations, I didn't leak her story," Feinstein told the commission, according to The Hill.
"She asked me to deal confidentially with it and I treated it confidentially, as she had asked."
But the president turned out not to believe her denial and he commented on it at the meeting on Saturday night: "I'll tell you something, that wasn't good body language.
