Elite League: Dundee Stars against Belfast Giants: 5-3
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
Dundee Stars have made up for Friday's loss in the Elite League against Belfast Giants by winning the return match 5-3 in Dundee on Saturday.
The Giants had names with two goals in the lead by scores from Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie brought the home team alongside, before Dwyer put the Giants back in the lead.
Francois Bouchard equalized for Dundee, before Lukas Lundvald Nielsen secured victory with two goals.
It was the third loss in the Elite League this season for the men of Adam Keefe, who had to make up for a backlog on Friday night to then beat Dundee 2-1 in Belfast.
It was the fourth time this season that the two teams met, with the Giants winning the previous three games.
Dwyer's opening goal came in the fourth minute at 3:35 on an assist from Kendall McFaull. David Rutherford gave the assist with which Beauvillier doubled the lead, four minutes later.
In a busy opening period, Sullivan brought the home team back into play at 13:10, before Matt Marquardt gave the lead for Cownie's equaliser at 15:16.
Dwyer ensured that the Giants went into first halftime with a lead, when he scored his second goal of the evening at the end of the first period.
The home team rebounded and Bouchard once again put them on a tie with a power-play goal at 27:37.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran then paired well to help Nielsen put Dundee in the lead for the first time in the match, late in the second period. He ensured victory with the fifth goal for his team, half way in the final period.
The Giants, who have now lost four of the last five games, are playing at home against Milton Keynes in their next game on Friday.
Air traffic controller dies, causing hundreds to flee the quake on an airplane
An air traffic controller in Indonesia is honored as a hero after he died because he ensured that a plane with hundreds of people on board could take off safely.
More than 800 people have been killed and many are still missing after a massive earthquake struck the island of Celebes on Friday; an earthquake that caused a tsunami afterwards.
Powerful aftershocks still plague the area and many are buried under the point in the city of Palu.
Despite the fact that his colleagues fled their lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to leave his post in the wild-back-and-forth-moving control tower of Mutiara Sis Al Jufri Airport Palu airport.
He remained to ensure that Batik Air's Flight 6321, which was ready at the runway at the time, could take off safely.
Then he jumped out of the control tower, when he thought it was about to collapse.
He later died at the hospital.
Yohannes Sirait, a spokesman for Air Navigation Indonesia, said his decision may have saved hundreds of lives, ABC News reported from Australia.
We had arranged a helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to take him to a larger hospital in another city.
Sadly, we lost him this morning, before the helicopter had reached Palu.
