Number of defectors in North Korea 'down' under Kim
According to a South Korean legislature, the number of North Korean defectors to South Korea has fallen since Kim Jong Un came to power seven years ago.
Park Byeong-seug, quoting data from the South Korean Ministry of Unification, states there were 1,127 defectors last year, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Mr Park noted that tougher border controls between North Korea and China, and higher penalties for people smugglers, are the main factors for the decline.
Pyongyang did not respond in public.
The vast majority of defectors from North Korea are eventually offered South Korean nationality.
According to sources in Seoul, more than 30,000 North Koreans have illegally crossed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most fled via China, which has the longest external border with North Korea and where you can make the crossing easier than in the high-security demilitarized zone (DMZ; Demilitarized Zone) between the two Koreas.
China considers the defectors as illegal migrants rather than refugees and often expels them by force.
Relations between the North and the South, which are still formally in a state of war, have improved significantly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang, mainly discussing the stagnated negotiations on denuclearization.
This meeting took place after the historic meeting between the president of the U.S., Donald Trump, and Kim Jong-un, in Singapore in June, which broadly corresponded to working towards the nuclear decommissioning of the Korean Peninsula
On Saturday, however, North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho accused the U.S. of the lack of progress in lifting U.S. sanctions ever since.
“Without faith in U.S. act, confidence in our national security is also lacking, and under those circumstances there is no first step of unilateral disarmament,” Mr. Ri said during a speech to the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi calls Brett Kavanaugh 'hysterical', and finds him incapacitated to serve in Supreme Court
House Speaker Nancy Pelosi called candidate judge for U.S. Supreme Court Brett Kavanaugh "hysterical," and finds him incapacitated to serve in the Supreme Court
Pelosi made the remarks in an interview, Saturday during the 'Texas Tribune Festival' in Austin, Texas.
'I couldn't help thinking that if a woman behaved like that, one would call her 'hysterical',' Pelosi said of her response to Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Committee on Justice on Thursday.
Kavanaugh emotionally denied the allegations that he sexually abused Dr Christine Blasey Ford when they were both teenagers.
During his opening statement, Kavanaugh was very emotional; he almost screamed and was especially upset when he talked about his family and his high school days.
He also emphatically condemned the Democrats in the committee, calling the accusations against him "great-squeaky and coordinated character assassination," organized by liberals who were angry that Hillary Clinton lost the presidential election in 2016.
Pelosi stated that she felt that Kavanaugh's testimony showed that he was not fit to serve the Supreme Court because he was full of prejudice against the democrats.
“I think he disqualifies himself with his statements and by the way he speaks about the Clintons and democrats,” Pelosi said.
Pelosi protested when she was asked if she'd try to file an impeachment proceeding against Kavanaugh if he were appointed, and the Democrats would get the majority in the House of Deputies.
“I can say this about that; if he doesn’t tell the truth in Congress, or against the FBI, he’s not only unsuitable for the Supreme Court, but also for the court where he’s serving now,” Pelosi said.
Kavanaugh is currently a judge at the Court of Justice of the "Distriction of Columbia" (usually abbreviated D.C.) District Court of Appeal.
Pelosi added that she, as a Democrat, was concerned that Kavanaugh is going to speak out against the 'Affordable Care Act or Roe v Wade'. Kavanaugh, after all, is known as a conservative judge.
Kavanaugh circumvented questions about revising certain Supreme Court decisions at confirmation hearings.
“Don’t expect us to stand cheering if a hysterical, biased person is appointed to the Supreme Court,” Pelosi said.
Women have to give in to this.
It's a righteous tirade of months and years of pent-up anger overflowing, and she can only cry to get it out.
"We're going to cry when we get angry," Mrs. Steinem told me 45 years later.
'I don't think that's weird, do you?"
She continued: 'I have been greatly helped by a woman who was a supervisor somewhere and who told me that she too would cry when she got angry. She developed a method, in which she started crying every time she got angry. To the one she then spoke to, she said: 'You may think I'm sad, because I'm crying.
- I'm angry.'
And then she just kept crying.
I just thought that was brilliant.'
Tears are partially lawful as an expression of anger, because crying becomes materially misunderstood.
One of my best remembered memories from a former job, in a men-dominated office, is when I had to cry terribly because I was terribly angry. An older woman, a chilly business leader that everyone was a little afraid of, grabbed me in my neck skin and dragged me into a stairwell.
"Never show them that you're crying," she said to me.
'They don't know you're furious.
They think you're sad and are happy that they got you in the closet.
Patricia Schroeder, then a member of Congress in Colorado for Democrats, had worked for Gary Hart on his candidacy for the presidency.
In 1987, when Mr. Hart was embroiled in an extramarital affair aboard a boat called 'Monkey Business' (informally that means 'cat quarrel' or 'apen strokes') and withdrew from the race, the deeply disappointed Mrs Schroeder thought there was no reason why she would not run for herself.
'It wasn't an informed decision,' she told me, laughing 30 years later.
'There were already 7 other candidates in the race, and the last thing they needed was another candidate.
Someone was talking "Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs."
Because the campaign was already advanced, she was behind with fundraising, and so she solemnly promised not to join if she couldn't raise $2 million.
It's been a lost battle.
She discovered that some of her supporters, who would give to men 1000 dollars, wanted to give her only 250 dollars.
'Do they think maybe I'll get a discount?' she wondered.
When she gave her speech in which she announced that she would not start a formal campaign after all, she became so overwhelmed by feelings - of gratitude for the people who had supported her, of frustration because the system made it so hard to raise money, and to reach voters instead of delegates, and of anger about sexism - that she choked on it.
'It will have been thought that I had a nervous breakdown,' Mrs Schroeder recalled about how the media reacted to her.
'They'll have thought Kleenex was my company sponsor.
I still remember thinking, what will they put on my tombstone?
“She was crying?”
How U.S.-China Trade War Can Be Beneficial for Beijing
The opening salvos of the U.S.-China trade war were deafening, and while the battle is still far from being fought, a gap between the two countries, according to long-term experts, could be beneficial for Beijing.
Donald Trump, the U.S. president, issued the first warning earlier this year by raising taxes on key Chinese export products, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation became known this week when new effective, increased tax rates were unveiled for 200 billion dollars (150 billion euros) worth of goods; half of all goods incomeed from China the U.S.
Beijing has taken reprisals each time in kind, most recently by raising tariffs from 5 to 10% for 60 billion dollars worth of U.S. goods.
China has pledged to match the U.S
In order for Washington to bend, requests must be granted, but publicly bowing to the U.S. is far too painful for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
However, experts argue that if Beijing plays it smart, the trade war with the U.S. could positively support China in the longer term, because the mutual dependence of the two economies decreases.
“The fact that a quick political decision in Washington or Beijing could create conditions, which could put one of both countries into an economic free fall, is actually much more dangerous than spectators have previously acknowledged,” said Abigail Grace, an investigative officer of the “Center for a New American Security,” a think tank specializing in national security issues focused on Asia.
Syria 'ready' to allow refugees to return, foreign minister said
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is calling for help to rebuild the country that has been devastated by a more than seven-year war.
During his speech to the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem stated that conditions in his country are improving.
“Today, the situation on the ground is more stable and safer, thanks to the progress made in combating terrorism,” he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate areas devastated by terrorists, and to normalize the situation on the ground.
All circumstances are now in place for the voluntary return of refugees to the country they have had to leave because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures that threatened their daily lives.
The U.N. estimates more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled their country since the war began in 2011.
It's estimated that there are still about six million people living in the country who need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime would welcome aid in recovering the devastated country.
However, he stressed that Syria would not accept conditional assistance or aid from countries that had subsidised the uprising.
Europe wins the 'Ryder Cup' in Paris
The European team won the '2018 Ryder Cup' by beating the U.S. team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 on the 'Le Golf National' golf course southwest of Paris in France.
The U.S. has now lost six consecutive times on European soil, and hasn't won the Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe won the title when the team of Danish captain Thomas Bjorn gained the required 14.5 points to beat the U.S.
U.S. team's star, Phil Mickelson, who was wrestling almost the entire tournament, allowed his long stroke from the tee to end up in the water at par 3, hole 16, and admitted his loss from Francesco Molinari.
The Italian golfer, Molinari, shined in all his rounds and became one of the four players who once scored 5-0-0 since the tournament in its current form began in 1979.
American Jordan Spieth was kicked out by the European team's lowest, Thorbjorn Olesen from Denmark, with 5 holes down and playing 4 holes.
The highest-ranked player in the world ranking, Dustin Johnson, lost 2 holes down and 1 hole to play from Ian Poulter from Britain, who may have played his last game in the Ryder Cup.
The Spaniard Segrio Garcia, veteran with eight Ryder Cups, became the best European player of all tournaments ever with 25.5 points in his career.
'I don't cry so quickly, but I can't do anything about it today.
- It's been a difficult year.
I am so grateful to Thomas that he selected me and believed in me.
I'm so glad we've got the cup back.
It's about the team, and I'm thrilled I was able to help', an emotional Garcia said after the European victory.
He passes the torch to his compatriot, John Ram, who beat American legend Tiger Woods with 2 holes down and 1 hole in the game for singles on Sunday.
'The enormous pride I feel because I've beaten Tiger Woods. I kept following that guy growing up,' the 23-year-old Rahm said.
Woods lost all four games in France and now has a record 13-21-3 in the Ryder Cup.
A strange statistic of one of the biggest players ever; winner of 14 major championships, second only behind Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team wrestled the weekend to find the fairways, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who played at a high level throughout the tournament.
The U.S. captain, Jim Furyk, said after the disappointing performance of his squad: 'I'm proud of these guys, they've struggled.
For a short period of time this morning we have made Europe a bit more difficult.
We offered opposition.
All due respect to Thomas.
- He's a great captain.
All 12 players on his team played very well.
We’re going to regroup. I’m going to work with the American PGA (Professional Golfers’ Association) and our Ryder Cup-Committee and we’re looking ahead.
I love these 12 guys, and I'm proud to be their captain.
You have to take your hat away from the opponent.
We've been overplayed.'
Red glow update: Concentrations decline in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota provinces
The most recent report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Conservation Commission (Florida Commission for the Conservation of Fish and Fauna) shows an overall decline in concentrations of Red Glow in parts of Tampa Bay Area (located on the west coast of Florida in the U.S.).
According to the commission, it is reported on fragmentary increase of the Red glow in the provinces of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, which could indicate a decrease in concentrations.
The Red Glow stretches over an average of 200 km of coastline, from northern Pinellas to the southern Lee counties.
There are about 16 km off the coast of Hillshoord County spots, but in fewer places than last week.
There's also Red Glow in Pasco County.
There are medium concentrations reported off the coast of Pinellas County this past week, low to high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, background to high concentrations in Manatee County, background to high concentrations at or off the coast of Sarasota County, background to high concentrations in Charlotte County, background to high concentrations at or off the coast of Lee County, and low concentrations in Collier County.
There is permanent reporting of respiratory irritation in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee, and Collier provinces.
In northwest Florida there was no mention of respiratory irritation over the past week.
